{"config": "ornith-d128-f16-rb4096", "task_id": "lru-cache", "type": "ts-tdd", "category": "sonnet", "rep": 0, "response": "<think>The user wants me to implement a generic LRU cache with per-entry TTL in TypeScript, following specific coding rules.\n\nLet me break down the requirements:\n1. Generic LRU cache with TTL\n2. Interface: `LruCache<K, V>` with `get`, `set`, `has`, `size`\n3. Must reuse `Clock` from `../../lib/clock`\n4. Strict generics, explicit return types, `readonly` on size, no `any`, no `!`\n5. Keep functions under 20 lines\n6. Use vitest for tests\n7. BDD naming: `should <behavior> when <condition>`\n8. Inject fake clock for TTL determinism\n9. Cover: eviction order, expiry, recency-refresh, capacity edge\n\nLet me think about the implementation:\n\n1. I need a `Map` or similar structure to store entries with their TTL info\n2. For LRU, I need to track access order\n3. When getting, I need to check if expired, and if not, refresh recency\n4. When setting, if at capacity, evict least recently used (non-expired)\n5. `size` should only count non-expired entries\n\nLet me think about the data structure:\n- I'll use a `Map<K, { value: V; insertedAt: number; accessedAt: number }>`\n- For LRU ordering, I can use the Map's iteration order (insertion order)\n- When accessing, I need to move the entry to the end (most recent)\n\nWait, Maps in JavaScript maintain insertion order. I can use that for LRU by deleting and re-inserting on access.\n\nLet me draft the implementation:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { Clock } from '../../lib/clock';\n\nexport interface LruCache<K, V> {\n  get(key: K): V | undefined;\n  set(key: K, value: V): void;\n  has(key: K): boolean;\n  readonly size: number;\n}\n\nexport const createLruCache = <K, V>(\n  capacity: number,\n  ttlMs: number,\n  clock: Clock\n): LruCache<K, V> => {\n  const store = new Map<K, { value: V; insertedAt: number; accessedAt: number }>();\n\n  const isExpired = (entry: { insertedAt: number; accessedAt: number }): boolean => {\n    return clock.now() >= entry.insertedAt + ttlMs;\n  };\n\n  const refreshRecency = (key: K): void => {\n    const entry = store.get(key);\n    if (entry) {\n      store.delete(key);\n      store.set(key, { ...entry, accessedAt: clock.now() });\n    }\n  };\n\n  const evictIfNeeded = (): void => {\n    if (store.size >= capacity) {\n      // Find and remove the oldest non-expired entry\n      const entries = Array.from(store.entries());\n      for (const [key, entry] of entries) {\n        if (!isExpired(entry)) {\n          store.delete(key);\n          return;\n        }\n      }\n      // If all expired, just clear and return\n      store.clear();\n    }\n  };\n\n  return {\n    get: (key: K): V | undefined => {\n      const entry = store.get(key);\n      if (!entry || isExpired(entry)) {\n        store.delete(key);\n        return undefined;\n      }\n      refreshRecency(key);\n      return entry.value;\n    },\n\n    set: (key: K, value: V): void => {\n      const existing = store.get(key);\n      if (existing) {\n        // Update existing entry\n        store.delete(key);\n        store.set(key, { value, insertedAt: existing.insertedAt, accessedAt: clock.now() });\n      } else {\n        evictIfNeeded();\n        store.set(key, { value, insertedAt: clock.now(), accessedAt: clock.now() });\n      }\n    },\n\n    has: (key: K): boolean => {\n      const entry = store.get(key);\n      if (!entry || isExpired(entry)) {\n        if (entry) store.delete(key);\n        return false;\n      }\n      refreshRecency(key);\n      return true;\n    },\n\n    get size(): number {\n      // Count non-expired entries\n      let count = 0;\n      for (const entry of store.values()) {\n        if (!isExpired(entry)) count++;\n      }\n      return count;\n    },\n  };\n};\n```\n\nWait, I need to check the rules again:\n- Keep every function under 20 lines \u2014 split eviction / expiry / recency into helpers.\n- No `any`, no `!`\n- Strict generics\n\nLet me refine:\n\nThe `get` function has a bit more logic. Let me split it better.\n\nAlso, I need to think about the `size` getter. It should be `readonly` and count only live entries.\n\nLet me also think about the eviction strategy more carefully:\n- When we need to evict, we should evict the least recently used (oldest `accessedAt`)\n- But we should skip expired entries\n\nActually, I think the current approach is fine. Let me refine the implementation to be cleaner and ensure all functions are under 20 lines.\n\nNow for tests, I need to cover:\n1. Eviction order (LRU behavior)\n2. Expiry (TTL behavior)\n3. Recency refresh (get refreshes access time)\n4. Capacity edge cases\n\nLet me draft the tests:\n\n```typescript\nimport { describe, expect, it, vi } from 'vitest';\nimport { createLruCache } from './impl';\nimport { systemClock } from '../../lib/clock';\n\nconst makeFakeClock = (initial: number) => {\n  let time = initial;\n  return {\n    now: () => time,\n    advance: (ms: number) => { time += ms; },\n  };\n};\n\ndescribe('createLruCache', () => {\n  describe('basic operations', () => {\n    it('should return undefined for missing key', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, systemClock);\n      expect(cache.get('missing')).toBeUndefined();\n    });\n\n    it('should store and retrieve a value', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, systemClock);\n      cache.set('key', 42);\n      expect(cache.get('key')).toBe(42);\n    });\n\n    it('should return true for existing key', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, systemClock);\n      cache.set('key', 42);\n      expect(cache.has('key')).toBe(true);\n    });\n\n    it('should return false for missing key', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, systemClock);\n      expect(cache.has('missing')).toBe(false);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('LRU eviction', () => {\n    it('should evict least recently used when at capacity', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 10000, systemClock);\n      cache.set('a', 1);\n      cache.set('b', 2);\n      cache.set('c', 3); // should evict 'a'\n      expect(cache.get('a')).toBeUndefined();\n      expect(cache.get('b')).toBe(2);\n      expect(cache.get('c')).toBe(3);\n    });\n\n    it('should respect recency when evicting', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 10000, systemClock);\n      cache.set('a', 1);\n      cache.set('b', 2);\n      cache.get('a'); // refresh recency\n      cache.set('c', 3); // should evict 'b'\n      expect(cache.get('a')).toBe(1);\n      expect(cache.get('b')).toBeUndefined();\n      expect(cache.get('c')).toBe(3);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('TTL expiry', () => {\n    it('should return undefined for expired entry', () => {\n      const clock = makeFakeClock(0);\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, clock);\n      cache.set('key', 42);\n      clock.advance(1001);\n      expect(cache.get('key')).toBeUndefined();\n    });\n\n    it('should not count expired entries in size', () => {\n      const clock = makeFakeClock(0);\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, clock);\n      cache.set('key', 42);\n      clock.advance(1001);\n      expect(cache.size).toBe(0);\n    });\n\n    it('should return false from has for expired entry', () => {\n      const clock = makeFakeClock(0);\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, clock);\n      cache.set('key', 42);\n      clock.advance(1001);\n      expect(cache.has('key')).toBe(false);\n    });\n\n    it('should refresh recency on get even if entry is near expiry', () => {\n      const clock = makeFakeClock(0);\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, clock);\n      cache.set('key', 42);\n      clock.advance(999);\n      cache.get('key'); // refresh recency\n      clock.advance(1); // now 1000ms total, but recency was at 999, so expires at 1999\n      expect(cache.get('key')).toBe(42);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('capacity edge cases', () => {\n    it('should handle capacity of 1', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(1, 10000, systemClock);\n      cache.set('a', 1);\n      cache.set('b', 2);\n      expect(cache.get('a')).toBeUndefined();\n      expect(cache.get('b')).toBe(2);\n    });\n\n    it('should update existing key without affecting LRU order', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 10000, systemClock);\n      cache.set('a', 1);\n      cache.set('b', 2);\n      cache.set('a', 10); // update existing\n      cache.set('c', 3); // should evict 'b'\n      expect(cache.get('a')).toBe(10);\n      expect(cache.get('b')).toBeUndefined();\n      expect(cache.get('c')).toBe(3);\n    });\n\n    it('should handle empty cache', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 10000, systemClock);\n      expect(cache.size).toBe(0);\n      expect(cache.has('any')).toBe(false);\n    });\n  });\n});\n```\n\nWait, I need to check if I'm following all the rules:\n- Reuse `Clock` from `../../lib/clock` \u2713\n- Strict generics \u2713\n- Explicit return types on exported fns \u2713\n- `readonly` on the public `size` \u2713\n- NO `any`, no `!` \u2713\n- Keep every function under 20 lines \u2713 (need to verify)\n- vitest tests only \u2713\n- BDD names `should <behavior> when <condition>` - I need to fix this\n\nLet me fix the test names to follow BDD style:\n\n```typescript\ndescribe('createLruCache', () => {\n  describe('basic operations', () => {\n    it('should return undefined when key is missing', () => {\n      // ...\n    });\n    // ...\n  });\n});\n```\n\nActually, looking at the rule more carefully: `BDD names should <behavior> when <condition>`. So:\n- `should return undefined when key is missing`\n- `should store and retrieve a value`\n- `should evict least recently used when at capacity`\n- etc.\n\nLet me refine the implementation to ensure all functions are under 20 lines and check for any `!` or `any`.\n\nLooking at my implementation, I see I'm using `!` in comments but not in code. Let me double-check the actual code... I don't see any `!` or `any` in the implementation.\n\nLet me also make sure the `size` getter is properly defined as `readonly`.\n\nActually, I realize I should double-check the `evictIfNeeded` function. It's a bit complex. Let me simplify:\n\n```typescript\nconst evictIfNeeded = (): void => {\n  if (store.size < capacity) return;\n  \n  // Collect non-expired entries\n  const liveEntries: [K, { value: V; insertedAt: number; accessedAt: number }][] = [];\n  for (const [key, entry] of store) {\n    if (!isExpired(entry)) {\n      liveEntries.push([key, entry]);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // If all expired, clear\n  if (liveEntries.length === 0) {\n    store.clear();\n    return;\n  }\n  \n  // Sort by accessedAt (oldest first) and evict\n  liveEntries.sort((a, b) => a[1].accessedAt - b[1].accessedAt);\n  store.delete(liveEntries[0][0]);\n};\n```\n\nThis is cleaner and still under 20 lines.\n\nActually, I realize I need to be more careful about the `size` getter. It should count only non-expired entries. Let me verify my implementation handles this correctly.\n\nLooking at my code, the `size` getter iterates through all entries and counts only those that are not expired. That's correct.\n\nNow let me think about edge cases:\n1. What if capacity is 0? Should we handle this?\n2. What if ttlMs is 0? Should entries expire immediately?\n\nFor now, I'll assume capacity > 0 and ttlMs >= 0.\n\nLet me also think about the `set` method. When updating an existing key, I should preserve the original `insertedAt` but refresh `accessedAt`. That's what my current implementation does.\n\nActually, I realize there's a subtle issue. When I call `set` on an existing key, I'm deleting and re-inserting. But I should preserve the original `insertedAt` to maintain the TTL. Let me check my implementation... Yes, I'm doing that: `{ value, insertedAt: existing.insertedAt, accessedAt: clock.now() }`.\n\nGood. Now let me finalize the implementation and tests.\n\nOne more thing: I need to make sure I'm not using `!` or `any` anywhere. Let me scan through my code... I don't see any.\n\nLet me also make sure all exported functions have explicit return types. My `createLruCache` function returns `LruCache<K, V>`, which is explicit.\n\nNow let me write the final implementation and tests.\n</think>// FILE: impl.ts\n```typescript\nimport type { Clock } from '../../lib/clock';\n\nexport interface LruCache<K, V> {\n  get(key: K): V | undefined;\n  set(key: K, value: V): void;\n  has(key: K): boolean;\n  readonly size: number;\n}\n\nexport const createLruCache = <K, V>(\n  capacity: number,\n  ttlMs: number,\n  clock: Clock\n): LruCache<K, V> => {\n  const store = new Map<K, { value: V; insertedAt: number; accessedAt: number }>();\n\n  const isExpired = (entry: { insertedAt: number; accessedAt: number }): boolean => {\n    return clock.now() >= entry.insertedAt + ttlMs;\n  };\n\n  const refreshRecency = (key: K): void => {\n    const entry = store.get(key);\n    if (entry) {\n      store.delete(key);\n      store.set(key, { ...entry, accessedAt: clock.now() });\n    }\n  };\n\n  const evictIfNeeded = (): void => {\n    if (store.size < capacity) return;\n\n    const liveEntries: [K, { value: V; insertedAt: number; accessedAt: number }][] = [];\n    for (const [key, entry] of store) {\n      if (!isExpired(entry)) {\n        liveEntries.push([key, entry]);\n      }\n    }\n\n    if (liveEntries.length === 0) {\n      store.clear();\n      return;\n    }\n\n    liveEntries.sort((a, b) => a[1].accessedAt - b[1].accessedAt);\n    store.delete(liveEntries[0][0]);\n  };\n\n  return {\n    get: (key: K): V | undefined => {\n      const entry = store.get(key);\n      if (!entry || isExpired(entry)) {\n        store.delete(key);\n        return undefined;\n      }\n      refreshRecency(key);\n      return entry.value;\n    },\n\n    set: (key: K, value: V): void => {\n      const existing = store.get(key);\n      if (existing) {\n        store.delete(key);\n        store.set(key, { value, insertedAt: existing.insertedAt, accessedAt: clock.now() });\n      } else {\n        evictIfNeeded();\n        store.set(key, { value, insertedAt: clock.now(), accessedAt: clock.now() });\n      }\n    },\n\n    has: (key: K): boolean => {\n      const entry = store.get(key);\n      if (!entry || isExpired(entry)) {\n        if (entry) store.delete(key);\n        return false;\n      }\n      refreshRecency(key);\n      return true;\n    },\n\n    get size(): number {\n      let count = 0;\n      for (const entry of store.values()) {\n        if (!isExpired(entry)) count++;\n      }\n      return count;\n    },\n  };\n};\n```\n\n// FILE: impl.test.ts\n```typescript\nimport { describe, expect, it, vi } from 'vitest';\nimport { createLruCache } from './impl';\nimport { systemClock } from '../../lib/clock';\n\nconst makeFakeClock = (initial: number) => {\n  let time = initial;\n  return {\n    now: (): number => time,\n    advance: (ms: number): void => { time += ms; },\n  };\n};\n\ndescribe('createLruCache', () => {\n  describe('basic operations', () => {\n    it('should return undefined when key is missing', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, systemClock);\n      expect(cache.get('missing')).toBeUndefined();\n    });\n\n    it('should store and retrieve a value', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, systemClock);\n      cache.set('key', 42);\n      expect(cache.get('key')).toBe(42);\n    });\n\n    it('should return true when key exists', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, systemClock);\n      cache.set('key', 42);\n      expect(cache.has('key')).toBe(true);\n    });\n\n    it('should return false when key is missing', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, systemClock);\n      expect(cache.has('missing')).toBe(false);\n    });\n\n    it('should report size of 0 for empty cache', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, systemClock);\n      expect(cache.size).toBe(0);\n    });\n\n    it('should increment size on set', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, systemClock);\n      cache.set('a', 1);\n      cache.set('b', 2);\n      expect(cache.size).toBe(2);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('LRU eviction', () => {\n    it('should evict least recently used when at capacity', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 10000, systemClock);\n      cache.set('a', 1);\n      cache.set('b', 2);\n      cache.set('c', 3);\n      expect(cache.get('a')).toBeUndefined();\n      expect(cache.get('b')).toBe(2);\n      expect(cache.get('c')).toBe(3);\n    });\n\n    it('should respect recency when evicting', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 10000, systemClock);\n      cache.set('a', 1);\n      cache.set('b', 2);\n      cache.get('a');\n      cache.set('c', 3);\n      expect(cache.get('a')).toBe(1);\n      expect(cache.get('b')).toBeUndefined();\n      expect(cache.get('c')).toBe(3);\n    });\n\n    it('should evict oldest accessed entry when multiple are stale', () => {\n      const clock = makeFakeClock(0);\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 10000, clock);\n      cache.set('a', 1);\n      cache.set('b', 2);\n      clock.advance(100);\n      cache.get('b');\n      clock.advance(100);\n      cache.set('c', 3);\n      expect(cache.get('a')).toBeUndefined();\n      expect(cache.get('b')).toBe(2);\n      expect(cache.get('c')).toBe(3);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('TTL expiry', () => {\n    it('should return undefined when entry is expired', () => {\n      const clock = makeFakeClock(0);\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, clock);\n      cache.set('key', 42);\n      clock.advance(1001);\n      expect(cache.get('key')).toBeUndefined();\n    });\n\n    it('should not count expired entries in size', () => {\n      const clock = makeFakeClock(0);\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, clock);\n      cache.set('key', 42);\n      clock.advance(1001);\n      expect(cache.size).toBe(0);\n    });\n\n    it('should return false from has when entry is expired', () => {\n      const clock = makeFakeClock(0);\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, clock);\n      cache.set('key', 42);\n      clock.advance(1001);\n      expect(cache.has('key')).toBe(false);\n    });\n\n    it('should refresh recency on get to extend TTL', () => {\n      const clock = makeFakeClock(0);\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, clock);\n      cache.set('key', 42);\n      clock.advance(999);\n      cache.get('key');\n      clock.advance(1);\n      expect(cache.get('key')).toBe(42);\n    });\n\n    it('should expire entry exactly at TTL boundary', () => {\n      const clock = makeFakeClock(0);\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, clock);\n      cache.set('key', 42);\n      clock.advance(1000);\n      expect(cache.get('key')).toBeUndefined();\n    });\n\n    it('should keep entry alive just before TTL boundary', () => {\n      const clock = makeFakeClock(0);\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, clock);\n      cache.set('key', 42);\n      clock.advance(999);\n      expect(cache.get('key')).toBe(42);\n    });\n\n    it('should expire all entries when all TTLs have elapsed', () => {\n      const clock = makeFakeClock(0);\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(3, 1000, clock);\n      cache.set('a', 1);\n      cache.set('b', 2);\n      cache.set('c', 3);\n      clock.advance(1001);\n      expect(cache.size).toBe(0);\n      expect(cache.has('a')).toBe(false);\n      expect(cache.has('b')).toBe(false);\n      expect(cache.has('c')).toBe(false);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('recency refresh', () => {\n    it('should refresh recency on get', () => {\n      const clock = makeFakeClock(0);\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(3, 10000, clock);\n      cache.set('a', 1);\n      cache.set('b', 2);\n      cache.set('c', 3);\n      clock.advance(100);\n      cache.get('a');\n      clock.advance(100);\n      cache.set('d', 4);\n      expect(cache.get('a')).toBe(1);\n      expect(cache.get('b')).toBeUndefined();\n    });\n\n    it('should refresh recency on has', () => {\n      const clock = makeFakeClock(0);\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(3, 10000, clock);\n      cache.set('a', 1);\n      cache.set('b', 2);\n      cache.set('c', 3);\n      clock.advance(100);\n      cache.has('a');\n      clock.advance(100);\n      cache.set('d', 4);\n      expect(cache.has('a')).toBe(true);\n      expect(cache.has('b')).toBe(false);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('capacity edge cases', () => {\n    it('should handle capacity of 1', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(1, 10000, systemClock);\n      cache.set('a', 1);\n      cache.set('b', 2);\n      expect(cache.get('a')).toBeUndefined();\n      expect(cache.get('b')).toBe(2);\n      expect(cache.size).toBe(1);\n    });\n\n    it('should update existing key without affecting LRU order', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 10000, systemClock);\n      cache.set('a', 1);\n      cache.set('b', 2);\n      cache.set('a', 10);\n      cache.set('c', 3);\n      expect(cache.get('a')).toBe(10);\n      expect(cache.get('b')).toBeUndefined();\n      expect(cache.get('c')).toBe(3);\n    });\n\n    it('should refresh recency when updating existing key', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 10000, systemClock);\n      cache.set('a', 1);\n      cache.set('b', 2);\n      cache.set('a', 10);\n      cache.set('c', 3);\n      expect(cache.get('a')).toBe(10);\n      expect(cache.get('b')).toBeUndefined();\n      expect(cache.get('c')).toBe(3);\n    });\n\n    it('should handle setting same key multiple times', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 10000, systemClock);\n      cache.set('a', 1);\n      cache.set('a', 2);\n      cache.set('a', 3);\n      expect(cache.get('a')).toBe(3);\n      expect(cache.size).toBe(1);\n    });\n\n    it('should clear expired entries before eviction', () => {\n      const clock = makeFakeClock(0);\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, clock);\n      cache.set('a', 1);\n      cache.set('b', 2);\n      clock.advance(1001);\n      cache.set('c', 3);\n      expect(cache.get('a')).toBeUndefined();\n      expect(cache.get('b')).toBeUndefined();\n      expect(cache.get('c')).toBe(3);\n      expect(cache.size).toBe(1);\n    });\n\n    it('should handle mixed expired and live entries during eviction', () => {\n      const clock = makeFakeClock(0);\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, clock);\n      cache.set('a', 1);\n      cache.set('b', 2);\n      clock.advance(500);\n      cache.set('c', 3);\n      clock.advance(600);\n      expect(cache.get('a')).toBeUndefined();\n      expect(cache.get('b')).toBe(2);\n      expect(cache.get('c')).toBe(3);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('generic types', () => {\n    it('should work with string keys and number values', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, number>(2, 1000, systemClock);\n      cache.set('a', 1);\n      expect(cache.get('a')).toBe(1);\n    });\n\n    it('should work with number keys and string values', () => {\n      const cache = createLruCache<number, string>(2, 1000, systemClock);\n      cache.set(1, 'one');\n      expect(cache.get(1)).toBe('one');\n    });\n\n    it('should work with object values', () => {\n      interface User {\n        id: number;\n        name: string;\n      }\n      const cache = createLruCache<string, User>(2, 1000, systemClock);\n      const user: User = { id: 1, name: 'Alice' };\n      cache.set('user-1', user);\n      expect(cache.get('user-1')).toEqual(user);\n    });\n  });\n});\n```", "prompt_tokens": 132796, "completion_tokens": 6941, "total_tokens": 139737, "cached_tokens": 0, "think_tokens": 3444, "answer_tokens": 3497, "prefill_tps": 1538.929774444854, "decode_tps": 68.22945776022007, "prompt_n": 132796, "prompt_ms": 86291.137, "predicted_n": 6941, "predicted_ms": 101730.253, "prompt_per_token_ms": 0.6498022304888702, "predicted_per_token_ms": 14.656426019305576, "draft_n": null, "draft_n_accepted": null, "draft_acceptance_pct": null, "ttft_s": 86.581, "ttfa_s": 136.913, "think_time_s": 50.332, "latency_s": 188.315, "finish_reason": "stop", "think_closed": true, "has_answer": true, "truncated_thinking": false, "sampling": {"temperature": 0.6, "top_p": 0.95, "top_k": 20, "seed": 42, "min_p": 0.0}, "max_tokens": 12288, "timings": {"cache_n": 0, "prompt_n": 132796, "prompt_ms": 86291.137, "prompt_per_token_ms": 0.6498022304888702, "prompt_per_second": 1538.929774444854, "predicted_n": 6941, "predicted_ms": 101730.253, "predicted_per_token_ms": 14.656426019305576, "predicted_per_second": 68.22945776022007}, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 6941, "prompt_tokens": 132796, "total_tokens": 139737, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 0}}, "ts": 1784480354.575691}
{"config": "ornith-d128-f16-rb4096", "task_id": "rate-limiter", "type": "ts-tdd", "category": "sonnet", "rep": 0, "response": "<think>The user wants me to implement a per-key token bucket rate limiter with lazy refill, using the existing `Clock` interface from `lib/clock.ts`.\n\nLet me analyze the requirements:\n1. `createRateLimiter(capacity: number, refillPerSec: number, clock: Clock): RateLimiter`\n2. Each key has its own bucket, starting FULL\n3. Lazy refill: on each call, compute `elapsedSeconds * refillPerSec`, cap at capacity\n4. `tryAcquire(key, tokens?)` returns true and deducts if enough tokens, else false\n5. Reuse `Clock` from `lib/clock`\n6. Rules: explicit return types, `readonly`, no `any`, no `!`, named constants, functions under 20 lines (split refill from acquire), vitest only, BDD test names, inject fake clock\n\nLet me design the implementation:\n\n```typescript\n// FILE: impl.ts\nimport type { Clock } from './lib/clock';\n\nconst DEFAULT_TOKENS = 1;\n\ninterface Bucket {\n  readonly tokens: number;\n  readonly lastRefill: number;\n}\n\ninterface RateLimiter {\n  readonly tryAcquire: (key: string, tokens?: number) => boolean;\n}\n\ninterface RateLimiterConfig {\n  readonly capacity: number;\n  readonly refillPerSec: number;\n}\n\nconst createBucket = (capacity: number, now: number): Bucket => ({\n  tokens: capacity,\n  lastRefill: now,\n});\n\nconst refill = (bucket: Bucket, now: number, refillPerSec: number): Bucket => {\n  const elapsed = (now - bucket.lastRefill) / 1000;\n  const added = elapsed * refillPerSec;\n  return {\n    tokens: Math.min(bucket.tokens + added, bucket.capacity),\n    lastRefill: now,\n  };\n};\n\nconst tryAcquire = (\n  config: RateLimiterConfig,\n  buckets: Map<string, Bucket>,\n  clock: Clock,\n  key: string,\n  tokens: number\n): boolean => {\n  const now = clock.now();\n  const existing = buckets.get(key);\n  const bucket = existing ?? createBucket(config.capacity, now);\n  const refilled = refill(bucket, now, config.refillPerSec);\n\n  if (refilled.tokens < tokens) {\n    buckets.set(key, refilled);\n    return false;\n  }\n\n  refilled.tokens -= tokens;\n  buckets.set(key, refilled);\n  return true;\n};\n\nexport const createRateLimiter = (\n  capacity: number,\n  refillPerSec: number,\n  clock: Clock\n): RateLimiter => {\n  const config: RateLimiterConfig = { capacity, refillPerSec };\n  const buckets = new Map<string, Bucket>();\n\n  return {\n    tryAcquire: (key: string, tokens?: number): boolean =>\n      tryAcquire(config, buckets, clock, key, tokens ?? DEFAULT_TOKENS),\n  };\n};\n```\n\nWait, the rules say \"Keep every function under 20 lines \u2014 split refill from acquire.\" I have split them. Let me check line counts:\n- `createBucket`: 4 lines\n- `refill`: 7 lines\n- `tryAcquire`: 13 lines\n- `createRateLimiter`: 9 lines\n\nAll under 20 lines. Good.\n\nNow the tests. I need to cover:\n- first-burst: can acquire up to capacity\n- lazy refill over time\n- empty-bucket rejection\n- per-key isolation\n\nLet me write the tests:\n\n```typescript\n// FILE: impl.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it, vi } from 'vitest';\n\nimport { systemClock } from './lib/clock';\n\nimport { createRateLimiter } from './impl';\n\n// Fake clock helper\nconst makeClock = (initial: number) => {\n  let now = initial;\n  return {\n    now: () => now,\n    advance: (ms: number) => {\n      now += ms;\n    },\n  };\n};\n\ndescribe('createRateLimiter \u2014 first-burst', () => {\n  it('should allow acquiring up to capacity when bucket is full', () => {\n    const clock = makeClock(0);\n    const limiter = createRateLimiter(5, 1, clock);\n\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n  });\n\n  it('should reject when bucket is empty', () => {\n    const clock = makeClock(0);\n    const limiter = createRateLimiter(2, 1, clock);\n\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(false);\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe('createRateLimiter \u2014 lazy refill', () => {\n  it('should refill tokens based on elapsed time', () => {\n    const clock = makeClock(0);\n    const limiter = createRateLimiter(10, 2, clock); // 2 tokens/sec\n\n    // Drain\n    for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) limiter.tryAcquire('k1');\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(false);\n\n    // Advance 1 second \u2192 refill 2 tokens\n    clock.advance(1000);\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(false);\n  });\n\n  it('should cap refill at capacity', () => {\n    const clock = makeClock(0);\n    const limiter = createRateLimiter(3, 10, clock); // 10 tokens/sec\n\n    // Drain\n    for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) limiter.tryAcquire('k1');\n\n    // Advance 5 seconds \u2192 would refill 50, but cap at 3\n    clock.advance(5000);\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(false);\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe('createRateLimiter \u2014 per-key isolation', () => {\n  it('should maintain independent buckets per key', () => {\n    const clock = makeClock(0);\n    const limiter = createRateLimiter(2, 1, clock);\n\n    // Drain key A\n    limiter.tryAcquire('a');\n    limiter.tryAcquire('a');\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('a')).toBe(false);\n\n    // Key B should still have tokens\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('b')).toBe(true);\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('b')).toBe(true);\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('b')).toBe(false);\n  });\n\n  it('should not affect one key when another key refills', () => {\n    const clock = makeClock(0);\n    const limiter = createRateLimiter(1, 1, clock);\n\n    limiter.tryAcquire('a'); // drain\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('a')).toBe(false);\n\n    clock.advance(1000); // refill key a's time, but don't call tryAcquire on a\n    // Now tryAcquire on b (which has its own bucket, full)\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('b')).toBe(true);\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe('createRateLimiter \u2014 multi-token acquire', () => {\n  it('should deduct specified tokens and reject if insufficient', () => {\n    const clock = makeClock(0);\n    const limiter = createRateLimiter(5, 1, clock);\n\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1', 3)).toBe(true);\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1', 3)).toBe(false); // only 2 left\n    expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1', 2)).toBe(true);\n  });\n});\n```\n\nWait, I should double-check the file paths. The task says \"Reuse the existing Clock from the codebase (`lib/clock`)\". The existing file is at `lib/clock.ts`. My impl.ts and impl.test.ts should be in the same directory or wherever. Let me use relative imports that make sense.\n\nActually, looking at the context, the user says \"Output exactly two files with `// FILE:` markers: `impl.ts` then `impl.test.ts`.\" I'll use relative imports assuming they're in the same directory.\n\nLet me refine the implementation to be cleaner and ensure all rules are followed:\n- Explicit return types \u2713\n- `readonly` where applicable \u2713\n- NO `any` \u2713\n- no `!` \u2713\n- named constants \u2713\n- functions under 20 lines \u2713\n- split refill from acquire \u2713\n- vitest only \u2713\n- BDD names `should <behavior> when <condition>` - I need to adjust test names to match this pattern more closely.\n\nLet me adjust test names:\n\n```\ndescribe('createRateLimiter', () => {\n  describe('tryAcquire', () => {\n    describe('first-burst', () => {\n      it('should allow acquiring up to capacity when bucket is full', () => { ... });\n      it('should reject when bucket is empty', () => { ... });\n    });\n    describe('lazy refill', () => {\n      it('should refill tokens based on elapsed time', () => { ... });\n      it('should cap refill at capacity', () => { ... });\n    });\n    describe('per-key isolation', () => {\n      it('should maintain independent buckets per key', () => { ... });\n      it('should not affect one key when another key refills', () => { ... });\n    });\n    describe('multi-token acquire', () => {\n      it('should deduct specified tokens and reject if insufficient', () => { ... });\n    });\n  });\n});\n```\n\nHmm, the BDD format requested is `should <behavior> when <condition>`. Let me make sure all `it()` names follow this:\n- \"should acquire tokens when bucket has sufficient capacity\"\n- \"should reject when bucket is empty\"\n- \"should refill tokens when elapsed time passes\"\n- \"should cap refill at capacity when elapsed time exceeds\"\n- \"should maintain independent buckets when keys differ\"\n- \"should not affect one key when another key is queried\"\n- \"should deduct specified tokens and reject when insufficient\"\n\nThese follow the pattern. Let me finalize.\n\nOne thing to verify: the `Clock` interface from `lib/clock.ts` has `now(): number`. My fake clock matches this.\n\nLet me also verify the `refill` logic:\n- `elapsed = (now - bucket.lastRefill) / 1000` \u2014 converts ms to seconds\n- `added = elapsed * refillPerSec` \u2014 tokens added\n- `tokens = Math.min(bucket.tokens + added, bucket.capacity)` \u2014 cap at capacity\n\nThis is correct.\n\nFor the first call with a new key, `bucket` is created with `tokens: capacity, lastRefill: now`. Then `refill` computes elapsed = 0, added = 0, so tokens stays at capacity. Good.\n\nLet me write the final version.\n</think>// FILE: impl.ts\nimport type { Clock } from './lib/clock';\n\nconst DEFAULT_TOKENS = 1;\nconst MS_PER_SEC = 1000;\n\ninterface Bucket {\n  readonly tokens: number;\n  readonly lastRefill: number;\n}\n\ninterface RateLimiterConfig {\n  readonly capacity: number;\n  readonly refillPerSec: number;\n}\n\ninterface RateLimiter {\n  readonly tryAcquire: (key: string, tokens?: number) => boolean;\n}\n\nconst createBucket = (capacity: number, now: number): Bucket => ({\n  tokens: capacity,\n  lastRefill: now,\n});\n\nconst refill = (\n  bucket: Bucket,\n  now: number,\n  refillPerSec: number\n): Bucket => {\n  const elapsed = (now - bucket.lastRefill) / MS_PER_SEC;\n  const added = elapsed * refillPerSec;\n  return {\n    tokens: Math.min(bucket.tokens + added, bucket.capacity),\n    lastRefill: now,\n  };\n};\n\nconst tryAcquire = (\n  config: RateLimiterConfig,\n  buckets: Map<string, Bucket>,\n  clock: Clock,\n  key: string,\n  tokens: number\n): boolean => {\n  const now = clock.now();\n  const existing = buckets.get(key);\n  const bucket = existing ?? createBucket(config.capacity, now);\n  const refilled = refill(bucket, now, config.refillPerSec);\n\n  if (refilled.tokens < tokens) {\n    buckets.set(key, refilled);\n    return false;\n  }\n\n  refilled.tokens -= tokens;\n  buckets.set(key, refilled);\n  return true;\n};\n\nexport const createRateLimiter = (\n  capacity: number,\n  refillPerSec: number,\n  clock: Clock\n): RateLimiter => {\n  const config: RateLimiterConfig = { capacity, refillPerSec };\n  const buckets = new Map<string, Bucket>();\n\n  return {\n    tryAcquire: (key: string, tokens?: number): boolean =>\n      tryAcquire(config, buckets, clock, key, tokens ?? DEFAULT_TOKENS),\n  };\n};\n\n\n// FILE: impl.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';\n\nimport { createRateLimiter } from './impl';\n\ninterface FakeClock {\n  readonly now: () => number;\n  readonly advance: (ms: number) => void;\n}\n\nconst makeClock = (initial: number): FakeClock => {\n  let now = initial;\n  return {\n    now: () => now,\n    advance: (ms: number) => {\n      now += ms;\n    },\n  };\n};\n\ndescribe('createRateLimiter', () => {\n  describe('tryAcquire \u2014 first-burst', () => {\n    it('should allow acquiring exactly capacity tokens when bucket starts full', () => {\n      const clock = makeClock(0);\n      const limiter = createRateLimiter(5, 1, clock);\n\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n    });\n\n    it('should reject when bucket is empty', () => {\n      const clock = makeClock(0);\n      const limiter = createRateLimiter(2, 1, clock);\n\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(false);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('tryAcquire \u2014 lazy refill', () => {\n    it('should refill tokens proportional to elapsed time', () => {\n      const clock = makeClock(0);\n      const limiter = createRateLimiter(10, 2, clock);\n\n      for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {\n        expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n      }\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(false);\n\n      clock.advance(1000);\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(false);\n    });\n\n    it('should cap refill at capacity when elapsed time would over-refill', () => {\n      const clock = makeClock(0);\n      const limiter = createRateLimiter(3, 10, clock);\n\n      for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n        limiter.tryAcquire('k1');\n      }\n\n      clock.advance(5000);\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(true);\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1')).toBe(false);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('tryAcquire \u2014 per-key isolation', () => {\n    it('should maintain independent buckets when keys differ', () => {\n      const clock = makeClock(0);\n      const limiter = createRateLimiter(2, 1, clock);\n\n      limiter.tryAcquire('a');\n      limiter.tryAcquire('a');\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('a')).toBe(false);\n\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('b')).toBe(true);\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('b')).toBe(true);\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('b')).toBe(false);\n    });\n\n    it('should not affect one key when another key is queried', () => {\n      const clock = makeClock(0);\n      const limiter = createRateLimiter(1, 1, clock);\n\n      limiter.tryAcquire('a');\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('a')).toBe(false);\n\n      clock.advance(1000);\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('b')).toBe(true);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('tryAcquire \u2014 multi-token', () => {\n    it('should deduct specified tokens and reject when insufficient', () => {\n      const clock = makeClock(0);\n      const limiter = createRateLimiter(5, 1, clock);\n\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1', 3)).toBe(true);\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1', 3)).toBe(false);\n      expect(limiter.tryAcquire('k1', 2)).toBe(true);\n    });\n  });\n});", "prompt_tokens": 132747, "completion_tokens": 4128, "total_tokens": 136875, "cached_tokens": 130744, "think_tokens": 2602, "answer_tokens": 1526, "prefill_tps": 951.5285738852674, "decode_tps": 69.20605151260571, "prompt_n": 2003, "prompt_ms": 2105.034, "predicted_n": 4128, "predicted_ms": 59647.963, "prompt_per_token_ms": 1.0509405891163255, "predicted_per_token_ms": 14.449603439922482, "draft_n": null, "draft_n_accepted": null, "draft_acceptance_pct": null, "ttft_s": 2.35, "ttfa_s": 39.878, "think_time_s": 37.528, "latency_s": 62.001, "finish_reason": "stop", "think_closed": true, "has_answer": true, "truncated_thinking": false, "sampling": {"temperature": 0.6, "top_p": 0.95, "top_k": 20, "seed": 42, "min_p": 0.0}, "max_tokens": 12288, "timings": {"cache_n": 130744, "prompt_n": 2003, "prompt_ms": 2105.034, "prompt_per_token_ms": 1.0509405891163255, "prompt_per_second": 951.5285738852674, "predicted_n": 4128, "predicted_ms": 59647.963, "predicted_per_token_ms": 14.449603439922482, "predicted_per_second": 69.20605151260571}, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 4128, "prompt_tokens": 132747, "total_tokens": 136875, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 130744}}, "ts": 1784480416.5839634}
{"config": "ornith-d128-f16-rb4096", "task_id": "async-memo", "type": "ts-tdd", "category": "opus", "rep": 0, "response": "<think>The user wants me to fix a buggy `createAsyncMemo` function and provide tests for it.\n\nRequirements:\n1. Dedup: concurrent calls for the same key share ONE in-flight computation\n2. Don't cache failures: if the computation rejects, it must not be cached \u2014 a later call retries `fn`\n3. Cache successes: once resolved, the value is reused \u2014 `fn` is not called again for that key\n\nCoding rules:\n- NO non-null `!` (the bug hint uses one \u2014 remove it)\n- NO `any`\n- explicit return types on every function/arrow\n- Keep every function under 20 lines\n- vitest tests only (no `jest.*`)\n- BDD names `should <behavior> when <condition>`\n- async tests must cover concurrent dedup, retry-after-rejection, and cache-hit-after-success\n\nLet me design the solution:\n\n```ts\nexport function createAsyncMemo<K, V>(fn: (key: K) => Promise<V>): (key: K) => Promise<V> {\n  const cache = new Map<K, Promise<V>>();\n  return async (key: K): Promise<V> => {\n    const existing = cache.get(key);\n    if (existing) return existing;\n    const p = fn(key).catch(() => {\n      cache.delete(key);\n      throw new Error();\n    });\n    cache.set(key, p);\n    return p;\n  };\n}\n```\n\nWait, I need to be careful. The `catch` handler throws a new error so that the promise rejects, but the cache entry is deleted. Let me think about this more carefully.\n\nActually, let me use a cleaner approach:\n\n```ts\nexport function createAsyncMemo<K, V>(fn: (key: K) => Promise<V>): (key: K) => Promise<V> {\n  const cache = new Map<K, Promise<V>>();\n  return async (key: K): Promise<V> => {\n    const existing = cache.get(key);\n    if (existing) return existing;\n    const p = fn(key);\n    p.catch(() => {\n      cache.delete(key);\n    });\n    cache.set(key, p);\n    return p;\n  };\n}\n```\n\nThis is cleaner. When `fn(key)` rejects, the `.catch` handler removes it from the cache. The promise still rejects to the caller. On next call, the cache doesn't have the key, so `fn` is called again.\n\nLet me verify the rules:\n- No `!` - check\n- No `any` - check\n- Explicit return types - check\n- Functions under 20 lines - check\n- vitest tests - check\n- BDD names - check\n- concurrent dedup, retry-after-rejection, cache-hit-after-success tests - check\n\nNow for tests, I need to cover:\n1. Concurrent dedup: multiple concurrent calls for same key \u2192 `fn` called once\n2. Retry after rejection: rejection doesn't cache, next call retries\n3. Cache hit after success: second call returns cached value without calling `fn` again\n\nLet me write the tests:\n\n```ts\nimport { describe, expect, it, vi } from 'vitest';\nimport { createAsyncMemo } from './impl';\n\ndescribe('createAsyncMemo', () => {\n  describe('should dedup concurrent calls when same key is requested', () => {\n    it('should call the source function only once for concurrent requests', async () => {\n      const fn = vi.fn(async (key: string) => key);\n      const memo = createAsyncMemo(fn);\n      \n      const p1 = memo('a');\n      const p2 = memo('a');\n      const p3 = memo('a');\n      \n      await Promise.all([p1, p2, p3]);\n      \n      expect(fn).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('should not cache failures when computation rejects', () => {\n    it('should retry the source function on next call after rejection', async () => {\n      const fn = vi.fn(async (_key: string) => {\n        throw new Error('fail');\n      });\n      const memo = createAsyncMemo(fn);\n      \n      await expect(memo('a')).rejects.toThrow('fail');\n      await expect(memo('a')).rejects.toThrow('fail');\n      \n      expect(fn).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(2);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('should cache successes when computation resolves', () => {\n    it('should not call the source function on subsequent calls with same key', async () => {\n      const fn = vi.fn(async (key: string) => key);\n      const memo = createAsyncMemo(fn);\n      \n      await memo('a');\n      const result = await memo('a');\n      \n      expect(fn).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);\n      expect(result).toBe('a');\n    });\n  });\n});\n```\n\nWait, I need to make sure the function is under 20 lines. Let me count:\n\n```ts\nexport function createAsyncMemo<K, V>(fn: (key: K) => Promise<V>): (key: K) => Promise<V> {\n  const cache = new Map<K, Promise<V>>();\n  return async (key: K): Promise<V> => {\n    const existing = cache.get(key);\n    if (existing) return existing;\n    const p = fn(key);\n    p.catch(() => {\n      cache.delete(key);\n    });\n    cache.set(key, p);\n    return p;\n  };\n}\n```\n\nThat's 11 lines. Good.\n\nLet me also make sure the tests are under 20 lines each. The test functions themselves are fine, but the describe blocks might push it. Let me restructure to keep individual test functions under 20 lines.\n\nActually, looking at the rule \"Keep every function under 20 lines\", I think this refers to the function implementations, not the test structure. But to be safe, let me keep everything concise.\n\nLet me finalize:\n\n```ts\n// FILE: impl.ts\nexport function createAsyncMemo<K, V>(fn: (key: K) => Promise<V>): (key: K) => Promise<V> {\n  const cache = new Map<K, Promise<V>>();\n  return async (key: K): Promise<V> => {\n    const existing = cache.get(key);\n    if (existing) return existing;\n    const p = fn(key);\n    p.catch(() => {\n      cache.delete(key);\n    });\n    cache.set(key, p);\n    return p;\n  };\n}\n```\n\n```ts\n// FILE: impl.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it, vi } from 'vitest';\nimport { createAsyncMemo } from './impl';\n\ndescribe('createAsyncMemo', () => {\n  describe('concurrent dedup', () => {\n    it('should call fn once when multiple concurrent requests share a key', async () => {\n      const fn = vi.fn(async (key: string) => key);\n      const memo = createAsyncMemo(fn);\n\n      const p1 = memo('a');\n      const p2 = memo('a');\n      const p3 = memo('a');\n\n      await Promise.all([p1, p2, p3]);\n\n      expect(fn).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('retry after rejection', () => {\n    it('should retry fn on next call when the previous computation rejected', async () => {\n      const fn = vi.fn(async (): Promise<never> => {\n        throw new Error('boom');\n      });\n      const memo = createAsyncMemo(fn);\n\n      await expect(memo('x')).rejects.toThrow('boom');\n      await expect(memo('x')).rejects.toThrow('boom');\n\n      expect(fn).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(2);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('cache hit after success', () => {\n    it('should return the cached value without calling fn again on subsequent requests', async () => {\n      const fn = vi.fn(async (key: string) => key);\n      const memo = createAsyncMemo(fn);\n\n      await memo('a');\n      const result = await memo('a');\n\n      expect(fn).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);\n      expect(result).toBe('a');\n    });\n  });\n});\n```\n\nLet me verify all requirements:\n1. Dedup: \u2713 - concurrent calls share one Promise\n2. Don't cache failures: \u2713 - `.catch` deletes from cache\n3. Cache successes: \u2713 - `cache.get(key)` returns existing promise\n4. No `!`: \u2713\n5. No `any`: \u2713\n6. Explicit return types: \u2713\n7. Functions under 20 lines: \u2713\n8. vitest only: \u2713\n9. BDD names: \u2713\n10. Tests cover concurrent dedup, retry-after-rejection, cache-hit-after-success: \u2713\n\nI think this is good. Let me output exactly the two files with `// FILE:` markers.\n</think>// FILE: impl.ts\nexport function createAsyncMemo<K, V>(fn: (key: K) => Promise<V>): (key: K) => Promise<V> {\n  const cache = new Map<K, Promise<V>>();\n  return async (key: K): Promise<V> => {\n    const existing = cache.get(key);\n    if (existing) return existing;\n    const p = fn(key);\n    p.catch(() => {\n      cache.delete(key);\n    });\n    cache.set(key, p);\n    return p;\n  };\n}\n\n// FILE: impl.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it, vi } from 'vitest';\nimport { createAsyncMemo } from './impl';\n\ndescribe('createAsyncMemo', () => {\n  describe('concurrent dedup', () => {\n    it('should call fn once when multiple concurrent requests share a key', async () => {\n      const fn = vi.fn(async (key: string) => key);\n      const memo = createAsyncMemo(fn);\n\n      const p1 = memo('a');\n      const p2 = memo('a');\n      const p3 = memo('a');\n\n      await Promise.all([p1, p2, p3]);\n\n      expect(fn).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('retry after rejection', () => {\n    it('should retry fn on next call when the previous computation rejected', async () => {\n      const fn = vi.fn(async (): Promise<never> => {\n        throw new Error('boom');\n      });\n      const memo = createAsyncMemo(fn);\n\n      await expect(memo('x')).rejects.toThrow('boom');\n      await expect(memo('x')).rejects.toThrow('boom');\n\n      expect(fn).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(2);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('cache hit after success', () => {\n    it('should return the cached value without calling fn again on subsequent requests', async () => {\n      const fn = vi.fn(async (key: string) => key);\n      const memo = createAsyncMemo(fn);\n\n      await memo('a');\n      const result = await memo('a');\n\n      expect(fn).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);\n      expect(result).toBe('a');\n    });\n  });\n});", "prompt_tokens": 132730, "completion_tokens": 2484, "total_tokens": 135214, "cached_tokens": 130744, "think_tokens": 1980, "answer_tokens": 504, "prefill_tps": 955.4484858835613, "decode_tps": 69.17837451707075, "prompt_n": 1986, "prompt_ms": 2078.605, "predicted_n": 2484, "predicted_ms": 35907.175, "prompt_per_token_ms": 1.0466289023162134, "predicted_per_token_ms": 14.455384460547505, "draft_n": null, "draft_n_accepted": null, "draft_acceptance_pct": null, "ttft_s": 2.317, "ttfa_s": 30.879, "think_time_s": 28.562, "latency_s": 38.228, "fini