{"config": "ornith-d128-f16-rb4096", "task_id": "expr-eval", "type": "ts-tdd", "category": "opus", "rep": 0, "response": "<think>Let me analyze the requirements carefully:\n\n1. Implement `evaluate(expr: string): Result` - a safe arithmetic expression evaluator\n2. Result type: `{ ok: true; value: number }` | `{ ok: false; error: string }`\n3. Supports: `+ - * /`, parentheses, arbitrary whitespace\n4. Standard precedence: `*`/`/` > `+`/-\n5. Left associativity: `10-3-2` = `5`, `8/2/2` = `2`\n6. On ANY malformed input, return `{ ok: false, error }` - never throw\n7. Rules:\n   - NO `any`, no non-null `!`\n   - Explicit return types\n   - `readonly` on Result fields\n   - Do NOT throw to caller - return Result\n   - Keep every function under 20 lines - split tokenizing / expr / term / factor\n   - No magic numbers beyond indices\n   - Vitest tests only\n   - BDD names `should <behavior> when <condition>`\n   - Cover precedence, left-associativity, parentheses, and at least one error case\n\nLet me design the implementation:\n\n**Tokenizer**: Convert string to tokens (numbers, operators, parens)\n**Parser**: Recursive descent parser with:\n  - `expr` \u2192 `term (('+' | '-') term)*`\n  - `term` \u2192 `factor (('*' | '/') factor)*`\n  - `factor` \u2192 number | '(' expr ')'\n\nLet me write the implementation:\n\n```typescript\n// FILE: impl.ts\n\nexport type Result =\n  | { readonly ok: true; readonly value: number }\n  | { readonly ok: false; readonly error: string };\n\ntype Token =\n  | { readonly kind: 'number'; readonly value: number }\n  | { readonly kind: 'plus' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'minus' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'star' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'slash' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'open' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'close' };\n\nconst tokenize = (input: string): Token[] => {\n  const tokens: Token[] = [];\n  let i = 0;\n  while (i < input.length) {\n    const ch = input[i];\n    if (ch === ' ' || ch === '\\t' || ch === '\\n' || ch === '\\r') {\n      i++;\n    } else if (ch >= '0' && ch <= '9') {\n      let start = i;\n      while (i < input.length && input[i] >= '0' && input[i] <= '9') i++;\n      tokens.push({ kind: 'number', value: Number(input.slice(start, i)) });\n    } else if (ch === '+') { tokens.push({ kind: 'plus' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '-') { tokens.push({ kind: 'minus' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '*') { tokens.push({ kind: 'star' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '/') { tokens.push({ kind: 'slash' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '(') { tokens.push({ kind: 'open' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === ')') { tokens.push({ kind: 'close' }); i++; }\n    else {\n      return []; // signal error\n    }\n  }\n  return tokens;\n};\n\n// Hmm, I need to signal error from tokenize. Let me use a different approach.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. I can have tokenize return `Result<Token[]>` or I can have the parser handle errors inline.\n\nLet me use a cleaner approach: tokenize returns an array, and we check for empty input as an error signal. But that's a magic number approach. Let me use a Result pattern throughout.\n\nActually, the spec says \"every function under 20 lines\". Let me keep it simple.\n\nLet me use a different approach: tokenize can return tokens or signal error via an empty array with a flag... No, that's messy.\n\nLet me have tokenize return `Result<Token[]>` and the parser functions also return `Result`.\n\nWait, but the spec says \"Keep every function under 20 lines\". Let me count carefully.\n\nLet me restructure:\n\n```typescript\nexport type Result =\n  | { readonly ok: true; readonly value: number }\n  | { readonly ok: false; readonly error: string };\n\ntype Token =\n  | { readonly kind: 'number'; readonly value: number }\n  | { readonly kind: 'plus' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'minus' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'star' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'slash' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'open' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'close' };\n\nconst tokenize = (input: string): Token[] | null => {\n  const tokens: Token[] = [];\n  let i = 0;\n  while (i < input.length) {\n    const ch = input[i];\n    if (ch === ' ' || ch === '\\t' || ch === '\\n' || ch === '\\r') {\n      i++;\n    } else if (ch >= '0' && ch <= '9') {\n      const start = i;\n      while (i < input.length && input[i] >= '0' && input[i] <= '9') i++;\n      tokens.push({ kind: 'number', value: Number(input.slice(start, i)) });\n    } else if (ch === '+') { tokens.push({ kind: 'plus' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '-') { tokens.push({ kind: 'minus' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '*') { tokens.push({ kind: 'star' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '/') { tokens.push({ kind: 'slash' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '(') { tokens.push({ kind: 'open' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === ')') { tokens.push({ kind: 'close' }); i++; }\n    else { return null; }\n  }\n  return tokens;\n};\n```\n\nHmm, but `null` is not great. Let me use a sentinel or just have the parser handle it.\n\nActually, let me think again. The spec says \"Do NOT throw to the caller \u2014 return a Result.\" So tokenize can throw internally (it's internal), and the top-level `evaluate` catches it and returns Result. But that's awkward.\n\nBetter: tokenize returns `Token[] | null` as a sentinel, and the parser checks for null.\n\nOr even better: tokenize returns `Result<Token[]>`.\n\nLet me just use a clean approach where tokenize returns `null` on error and the caller handles it. Actually no, let me use Result throughout for consistency.\n\nWait, the spec says \"every function under 20 lines\". Let me just use a simple approach: tokenize returns `Token[] | null`, and I document that null means error. Or I use Result.\n\nLet me go with Result for consistency:\n\n```typescript\nconst tokenize = (input: string): Result<Token[]> => {\n  const tokens: Token[] = [];\n  let i = 0;\n  while (i < input.length) {\n    const ch = input[i];\n    if (ch === ' ' || ch === '\\t' || ch === '\\n' || ch === '\\r') {\n      i++;\n    } else if (ch >= '0' && ch <= '9') {\n      const start = i;\n      while (i < input.length && input[i] >= '0' && input[i] <= '9') i++;\n      tokens.push({ kind: 'number', value: Number(input.slice(start, i)) });\n    } else if (ch === '+') { tokens.push({ kind: 'plus' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '-') { tokens.push({ kind: 'minus' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '*') { tokens.push({ kind: 'star' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '/') { tokens.push({ kind: 'slash' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '(') { tokens.push({ kind: 'open' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === ')') { tokens.push({ kind: 'close' }); i++; }\n    else {\n      return { ok: false, error: `Unexpected character: ${ch}` };\n    }\n  }\n  return { ok: true, value: tokens };\n};\n```\n\nHmm, but this makes the parser more complex. Let me use a simpler approach.\n\nActually, let me just use a cursor-based parser that tracks position and returns Result:\n\n```typescript\ntype ParserState = {\n  readonly tokens: readonly Token[];\n  readonly pos: number;\n};\n\nconst parseExpr = (state: ParserState): Result<{ value: number; pos: number }> => {\n  let left = yieldTerm(state);\n  if (left.ok) {\n    while (left.value.pos < left.value.tokens.length) {\n      const tok = left.value.tokens[left.value.pos];\n      if (tok.kind === 'plus' || tok.kind === 'minus') {\n        const op = tok.kind;\n        const right = yieldTerm({ tokens: left.value.tokens, pos: left.value.pos + 1 });\n        if (!right.ok) return right;\n        const r = right.value;\n        const newVal = op === 'plus' ? left.value.value + r.value : left.value.value - r.value;\n        left = { ok: true, value: { value: newVal, pos: r.pos } };\n      } else {\n        break;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  return left;\n};\n```\n\nThis is getting complex. Let me simplify.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest approach is:\n\n1. Tokenize returns `Token[]` or throws (internal error). The `evaluate` function wraps it.\n2. Parser functions take a cursor and return `Result<number>` with updated cursor position... \n\nActually, let me just go with a simpler design:\n\n```typescript\nexport type Result =\n  | { readonly ok: true; readonly value: number }\n  | { readonly ok: false; readonly error: string };\n\ntype Token =\n  | { readonly kind: 'number'; readonly value: number }\n  | { readonly kind: 'plus' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'minus' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'star' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'slash' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'open' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'close' };\n\nconst tokenize = (input: string): Token[] | null => {\n  const tokens: Token[] = [];\n  let i = 0;\n  while (i < input.length) {\n    const ch = input[i];\n    if (ch === ' ' || ch === '\\t' || ch === '\\n' || ch === '\\r') {\n      i++;\n    } else if (ch >= '0' && ch <= '9') {\n      const start = i;\n      while (i < input.length && input[i] >= '0' && input[i] <= '9') i++;\n      tokens.push({ kind: 'number', value: Number(input.slice(start, i)) });\n    } else if (ch === '+') { tokens.push({ kind: 'plus' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '-') { tokens.push({ kind: 'minus' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '*') { tokens.push({ kind: 'star' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '/') { tokens.push({ kind: 'slash' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '(') { tokens.push({ kind: 'open' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === ')') { tokens.push({ kind: 'close' }); i++; }\n    else { return null; }\n  }\n  return tokens;\n};\n\nconst parseExpr = (tokens: readonly Token[], pos: number): Result<{ value: number; pos: number }> => {\n  let left = parseTerm(tokens, pos);\n  if (!left.ok) return left;\n  let current = left.value;\n  while (\n    current.pos < tokens.length &&\n    (tokens[current.pos].kind === 'plus' || tokens[current.pos].kind === 'minus')\n  ) {\n    const op = tokens[current.pos].kind;\n    const right = parseTerm(tokens, current.pos + 1);\n    if (!right.ok) return right;\n    const r = right.value;\n    const newVal = op === 'plus' ? current.value + r.value : current.value - r.value;\n    current = { value: newVal, pos: r.pos };\n  }\n  return { ok: true, value: current };\n};\n\nconst parseTerm = (tokens: readonly Token[], pos: number): Result<{ value: number; pos: number }> => {\n  let left = parseFactor(tokens, pos);\n  if (!left.ok) return left;\n  let current = left.value;\n  while (\n    current.pos < tokens.length &&\n    (tokens[current.pos].kind === 'star' || tokens[current.pos].kind === 'slash')\n  ) {\n    const op = tokens[current.pos].kind;\n    const right = parseFactor(tokens, current.pos + 1);\n    if (!right.ok) return right;\n    const r = right.value;\n    const newVal = op === 'star' ? current.value * r.value : current.value / r.value;\n    current = { value: newVal, pos: r.pos };\n  }\n  return { ok: true, value: current };\n};\n\nconst parseFactor = (tokens: readonly Token[], pos: number): Result<{ value: number; pos: number }> => {\n  if (pos >= tokens.length) return { ok: false, error: 'Unexpected end of expression' };\n  const tok = tokens[pos];\n  if (tok.kind === 'number') {\n    return { ok: true, value: { value: tok.value, pos: pos + 1 } };\n  }\n  if (tok.kind === 'open') {\n    const inner = parseExpr(tokens, pos + 1);\n    if (!inner.ok) return inner;\n    const next = inner.value.pos;\n    if (next >= tokens.length || tokens[next].kind !== 'close') {\n      return { ok: false, error: 'Missing closing parenthesis' };\n    }\n    return { ok: true, value: { value: inner.value.value, pos: next + 1 } };\n  }\n  return { ok: false, error: `Unexpected token: ${tok.kind}` };\n};\n\nexport const evaluate = (expr: string): Result => {\n  const tokens = tokenize(expr);\n  if (tokens === null) {\n    return { ok: false, error: 'Invalid character in expression' };\n  }\n  if (tokens.length === 0) {\n    return { ok: false, error: 'Empty expression' };\n  }\n  const result = parseExpr(tokens, 0);\n  if (!result.ok) return result;\n  if (result.value.pos !== tokens.length) {\n    return { ok: false, error: 'Unexpected token after expression' };\n  }\n  return { ok: true, value: result.value.value };\n};\n```\n\nWait, I need to be careful about division by zero. The spec says \"division by zero\" should return error. JavaScript's `Number` division by zero gives `Infinity` or `-Infinity`. I need to check for that.\n\nActually, let me check: `5 / 0` in JavaScript gives `Infinity`. So I should check if the result is `Infinity` or `NaN` after division.\n\nActually, I should check if the divisor is 0 specifically:\n\n```typescript\nif (op === 'slash' && r.value === 0) {\n  return { ok: false, error: 'Division by zero' };\n}\n```\n\nLet me also make sure the functions are under 20 lines each.\n\nLet me count lines:\n- `tokenize`: ~25 lines - too many. Let me split.\n- `parseExpr`: ~15 lines - OK\n- `parseTerm`: ~15 lines - OK\n- `parseFactor`: ~18 lines - OK\n- `evaluate`: ~15 lines - OK\n\nHmm, `tokenize` is too long. Let me split it into `tokenize` and `readNumber`.\n\nActually, let me re-read the spec: \"Keep every function under 20 lines \u2014 split tokenizing / expr / term / factor.\"\n\nSo the spec wants me to split into: tokenizing, expr, term, factor. That's 4 functions. Let me make tokenize shorter.\n\nActually, I think the intent is that I should have separate functions for tokenizing, expression parsing, term parsing, and factor parsing. The tokenize function itself can be a bit longer but should be reasonable.\n\nLet me split tokenize into `tokenize` and `nextToken` or something. Or I can use a helper for reading numbers.\n\nLet me restructure:\n\n```typescript\nconst readNumber = (input: string, i: number): { value: number; next: number } | null => {\n  const start = i;\n  while (i < input.length && input[i] >= '0' && input[i] <= '9') i++;\n  if (i === start) return null;\n  return { value: Number(input.slice(start, i)), next: i };\n};\n\nconst tokenize = (input: string): Token[] | null => {\n  const tokens: Token[] = [];\n  let i = 0;\n  while (i < input.length) {\n    const ch = input[i];\n    if (ch === ' ' || ch === '\\t' || ch === '\\n' || ch === '\\r') {\n      i++;\n    } else if (ch >= '0' && ch <= '9') {\n      const result = readNumber(input, i);\n      if (result === null) return null;\n      tokens.push({ kind: 'number', value: result.value });\n      i = result.next;\n    } else if (ch === '+') { tokens.push({ kind: 'plus' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '-') { tokens.push({ kind: 'minus' }); i</think>// FILE: impl.ts\n\nexport type Result =\n  | { readonly ok: true; readonly value: number }\n  | { readonly ok: false; readonly error: string };\n\ntype Token =\n  | { readonly kind: 'number'; readonly value: number }\n  | { readonly kind: 'plus' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'minus' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'star' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'slash' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'open' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'close' };\n\nconst readNumber = (input: string, i: number): { value: number; next: number } | null => {\n  const start = i;\n  while (i < input.length && input[i] >= '0' && input[i] <= '9') i++;\n  if (i === start) return null;\n  return { value: Number(input.slice(start, i)), next: i };\n};\n\nconst tokenize = (input: string): Token[] | null => {\n  const tokens: Token[] = [];\n  let i = 0;\n  while (i < input.length) {\n    const ch = input[i];\n    if (ch === ' ' || ch === '\\t' || ch === '\\n' || ch === '\\r') {\n      i++;\n    } else if (ch >= '0' && ch <= '9') {\n      const result = readNumber(input, i);\n      if (result === null) return null;\n      tokens.push({ kind: 'number', value: result.value });\n      i = result.next;\n    } else if (ch === '+') { tokens.push({ kind: 'plus' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '-') { tokens.push({ kind: 'minus' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '*') { tokens.push({ kind: 'star' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '/') { tokens.push({ kind: 'slash' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === '(') { tokens.push({ kind: 'open' }); i++; }\n    else if (ch === ')') { tokens.push({ kind: 'close' }); i++; }\n    else { return null; }\n  }\n  return tokens;\n};\n\nconst parseExpr = (tokens: readonly Token[], pos: number): Result<{ value: number; pos: number }> => {\n  let current = parseTerm(tokens, pos);\n  if (!current.ok) return current;\n  while (\n    current.value.pos < tokens.length &&\n    (tokens[current.value.pos].kind === 'plus' || tokens[current.value.pos].kind === 'minus')\n  ) {\n    const op = tokens[current.value.pos].kind;\n    const right = parseTerm(tokens, current.value.pos + 1);\n    if (!right.ok) return right;\n    const r = right.value;\n    const newVal = op === 'plus' ? current.value.value + r.value : current.value.value - r.value;\n    current = { ok: true, value: { value: newVal, pos: r.pos } };\n  }\n  return current;\n};\n\nconst parseTerm = (tokens: readonly Token[], pos: number): Result<{ value: number; pos: number }> => {\n  let current = parseFactor(tokens, pos);\n  if (!current.ok) return current;\n  while (\n    current.value.pos < tokens.length &&\n    (tokens[current.value.pos].kind === 'star' || tokens[current.value.pos].kind === 'slash')\n  ) {\n    const op = tokens[current.value.pos].kind;\n    const right = parseFactor(tokens, current.value.pos + 1);\n    if (!right.ok) return right;\n    const r = right.value;\n    if (op === 'slash' && r.value === 0) {\n      return { ok: false, error: 'Division by zero' };\n    }\n    const newVal = op === 'star' ? current.value.value * r.value : current.value.value / r.value;\n    current = { ok: true, value: { value: newVal, pos: r.pos } };\n  }\n  return current;\n};\n\nconst parseFactor = (tokens: readonly Token[], pos: number): Result<{ value: number; pos: number }> => {\n  if (pos >= tokens.length) {\n    return { ok: false, error: 'Unexpected end of expression' };\n  }\n  const tok = tokens[pos];\n  if (tok.kind === 'number') {\n    return { ok: true, value: { value: tok.value, pos: pos + 1 } };\n  }\n  if (tok.kind === 'open') {\n    const inner = parseExpr(tokens, pos + 1);\n    if (!inner.ok) return inner;\n    const next = inner.value.pos;\n    if (next >= tokens.length || tokens[next].kind !== 'close') {\n      return { ok: false, error: 'Missing closing parenthesis' };\n    }\n    return { ok: true, value: { value: inner.value.value, pos: next + 1 } };\n  }\n  return { ok: false, error: `Unexpected token: ${tok.kind}` };\n};\n\nexport const evaluate = (expr: string): Result => {\n  const tokens = tokenize(expr);\n  if (tokens === null) {\n    return { ok: false, error: 'Invalid character in expression' };\n  }\n  if (tokens.length === 0) {\n    return { ok: false, error: 'Empty expression' };\n  }\n  const result = parseExpr(tokens, 0);\n  if (!result.ok) return result;\n  if (result.value.pos !== tokens.length) {\n    return { ok: false, error: 'Unexpected token after expression' };\n  }\n  return { ok: true, value: result.value.value };\n};\n\n\n// FILE: impl.test.ts\n\nimport { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';\n\nimport { evaluate, type Result } from './impl';\n\nconst expectOk = (result: Result, expected: number): void => {\n  expect(result.ok).toBe(true);\n  if (result.ok) expect(result.value).toBe(expected);\n};\n\nconst expectErr = (result: Result): void => {\n  expect(result.ok).toBe(false);\n  if (!result.ok) expect(typeof result.error).toBe('string');\n};\n\ndescribe('evaluate', () => {\n  describe('basic arithmetic', () => {\n    it('should return 0 for an empty expression', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate(''));\n    });\n\n    it('should return the number for a single-digit literal', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('7'), 7);\n    });\n\n    it('should return the number for a multi-digit literal', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('1234'), 1234);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 15 for \"10 + 5\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('10 + 5'), 15);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 5 for \"10 - 5\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('10 - 5'), 5);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 50 for \"10 * 5\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('10 * 5'), 50);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 5 for \"10 / 2\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('10 / 2'), 5);\n    });\n\n    it('should handle leading whitespace', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('   42'), 42);\n    });\n\n    it('should handle trailing whitespace', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('42   '), 42);\n    });\n\n    it('should handle internal whitespace around operators', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('1  +  2'), 3);\n    });\n\n    it('should handle tabs and newlines as whitespace', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('1\\t+\\n2'), 3);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('precedence: * and / bind tighter than + and -', () => {\n    it('should return 14 for \"2 + 3 * 4\" (not 20)', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('2 + 3 * 4'), 14);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 14 for \"3 * 4 + 2\" (not 14)', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('3 * 4 + 2'), 14);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 2 for \"10 / 2 + 3\" (not 8)', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('10 / 2 + 3'), 2);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 11 for \"1 + 2 * 3 + 4\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('1 + 2 * 3 + 4'), 11);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 10 for \"2 * 3 + 4\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('2 * 3 + 4'), 10);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 10 for \"2 + 3 * 2\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('2 + 3 * 2'), 8);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('left associativity', () => {\n    it('should return 5 for \"10 - 3 - 2\" (left-assoc: (10-3)-2)', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('10 - 3 - 2'), 5);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 2 for \"8 / 2 / 2\" (left-assoc: (8/2)/2)', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('8 / 2 / 2'), 2);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 1 for \"10 - 3 - 2 - 4\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('10 - 3 - 2 - 4'), 1);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 1 for \"100 / 10 / 2 / 5\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('100 / 10 / 2 / 5'), 1);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 14 for \"2 + 3 + 4 + 5\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('2 + 3 + 4 + 5'), 14);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 12 for \"2 * 3 * 2\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('2 * 3 * 2'), 12);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('mixed precedence and associativity', () => {\n    it('should return 16 for \"2 + 3 * 4 - 2\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('2 + 3 * 4 - 2'), 12);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 14 for \"10 - 2 * 3\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('10 - 2 * 3'), 4);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 7 for \"1 + 2 * 3\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('1 + 2 * 3'), 7);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 8 for \"10 - 2 / 2 - 3\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('10 - 2 / 2 - 3'), 7);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('parentheses', () => {\n    it('should return 7 for \"(2 + 3) * 4\" overriding precedence', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('(2 + 3) * 4'), 20);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 20 for \"(2 + 3) * 4\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('(2 + 3) * 4'), 20);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 14 for \"((2 + 3) * 4) - 6\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('((2 + 3) * 4) - 6'), 14);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 5 for \"(1 + 2) + (3 + 4)\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('(1 + 2) + (3 + 4)'), 10);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 1 for \"(((1)))\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('(((1)))'), 1);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 30 for \"(2 + 3) * (4 + 2)\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('(2 + 3) * (4 + 2)'), 30);\n    });\n\n    it('should return -5 for \"(-3 + 2)\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('(-3 + 2)'), -1);\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('division by zero', () => {\n    it('should return error for \"10 / 0\"', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('10 / 0'));\n    });\n\n    it('should return error for \"0 / 0\"', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('0 / 0'));\n    });\n\n    it('should return error for \"5 / (2 - 2)\"', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('5 / (2 - 2)'));\n    });\n\n    it('should return error for \"10 / 0 / 2\"', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('10 / 0 / 2'));\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('error cases', () => {\n    it('should return error for unbalanced open parenthesis', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('(2 + 3'));\n    });\n\n    it('should return error for unbalanced close parenthesis', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('2 + 3)'));\n    });\n\n    it('should return error for nested unbalanced parenthesis', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('((2 + 3)'));\n    });\n\n    it('should return error for missing left operand \" + 5\"', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('+ 5'));\n    });\n\n    it('should return error for missing right operand \"5 +\"', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('5 +'));\n    });\n\n    it('should return error for missing right operand \"5 *\"', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('5 *'));\n    });\n\n    it('should return error for missing right operand \"5 /\"', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('5 /'));\n    });\n\n    it('should return error for missing right operand \"5 -\"', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('5 -'));\n    });\n\n    it('should return error for consecutive operators \"2 ++ 3\"', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('2 ++ 3'));\n    });\n\n    it('should return error for consecutive operators \"2 * / 3\"', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('2 * / 3'));\n    });\n\n    it('should return error for letter characters \"2 + a\"', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('2 + a'));\n    });\n\n    it('should return error for special characters \"2 + @\"', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('2 + @'));\n    });\n\n    it('should return error for only whitespace', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('   '));\n    });\n\n    it('should return error for only tabs', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('\\t\\t'));\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe('negative numbers and edge cases', () => {\n    it('should return -5 for \"-5\"', () => {\n      expectErr(evaluate('-5'));\n    });\n\n    it('should return 0 for \"0\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('0'), 0);\n    });\n\n    it('should return 100 for \"100\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('100'), 100);\n    });\n\n    it('should return -1 for \"0 - 1\"', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('0 - 1'), -1);\n    });\n\n    it('should handle deeply nested parentheses', () => {\n      expectOk(evaluate('((((1 + 2))))'), 3);\n    });\n  });\n});", "prompt_tokens": 132715, "completion_tokens": 7895, "total_tokens": 140610, "cached_tokens": 0, "think_tokens": 4095, "answer_tokens": 3800, "prefill_tps": 1525.2896915483022, "decode_tps": 67.97891604858735, "prompt_n": 132715, "prompt_ms": 87009.701, "predicted_n": 7895, "predicted_ms": 116138.951, "prompt_per_token_ms": 0.6556131635459443, "predicted_per_token_ms": 14.710443445218493, "draft_n": null, "draft_n_accepted": null, "draft_acceptance_pct": null, "ttft_s": 87.279, "ttfa_s": 147.226, "think_time_s": 59.947, "latency_s": 203.422, "finish_reason": "stop", "think_closed": true, "has_answer": true, "truncated_thinking": false, "sampling": {"temperature": 0.6, "top_p": 0.95, "top_k": 20, "seed": 42, "min_p": 0.0}, "max_tokens": 12288, "timings": {"cache_n": 0, "prompt_n": 132715, "prompt_ms": 87009.701, "prompt_per_token_ms": 0.6556131635459443, "prompt_per_second": 1525.2896915483022, "predicted_n": 7895, "predicted_ms": 116138.951, "predicted_per_token_ms": 14.710443445218493, "predicted_per_second": 67.97891604858735}, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 7895, "prompt_tokens": 132715, "total_tokens": 140610, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 0}}, "ts": 1784480795.498865}
{"config": "ornith-d128-f16-rb4096", "task_id": "pricing-deferred", "type": "ts-tdd", "category": "opus", "rep": 0, "response": "<think>Let me analyze the task carefully:\n\n1. Add a `deferred` variant to the `Amount` union in `types.ts`\n2. Make `lineTotal` and `computeTotal` async to handle deferred values\n3. Update `report.ts` to handle deferred (label as \"deferred\", resolve totals)\n4. Audit and fix `cache.ts` to satisfy the contract:\n   - Concurrent calls for same fresh key share ONE computation\n   - Failed computations are never cached\n   - TTL still works via Clock\n   - Different keys stay independent\n\nLet me trace through the code:\n\n**types.ts**: Add `deferred` variant to `Amount`\n\n**pricing.ts**: \n- `lineTotal` needs to become async (return `Promise<number>`)\n- `computeTotal` needs to become async\n- `pctOf` and `adjustmentOf` can stay sync, but `lineTotal` calls `load()` for deferred\n\n**report.ts**:\n- `amountLabel` needs to handle \"deferred\" \u2192 \"deferred\"\n- `reportLine` needs to be async (await lineTotal)\n- `buildReport` needs to be async (Promise.all)\n\n**cache.ts**: Current implementation has issues:\n- No deduplication of concurrent calls \u2014 each `get` call creates its own computation\n- No handling of failed computations (they'd be cached as expired entries? Actually they'd throw, but the entry wouldn't be set... wait, let me re-read)\n\nCurrent cache code:\n```ts\nasync get(key: string, compute: () => Promise<number>): Promise<number> {\n  const hit = entries.get(key);\n  if (hit !== undefined && hit.expiresAt > clock.now()) {\n    return hit.value;\n  }\n  const value = await compute();\n  entries.set(key, { value, expiresAt: clock.now() + ttlMs });\n  return value;\n}\n```\n\nIssues:\n1. **Concurrent calls**: If two `get` calls happen simultaneously for the same key, both will miss the cache, both will call `compute()`, both will set the entry. The contract says compute should be invoked exactly once.\n2. **Failed computation**: If `compute()` throws, the entry is never set, so a retry would work. That's actually OK for the failure case \u2014 but we need to make sure the entry is NOT cached on failure.\n3. **TTL**: Still works via Clock.\n4. **Different keys independent**: Yes, Map keys are independent.\n\nSo the main fix needed is concurrent deduplication.\n\nLet me design the cache fix:\n\n```ts\nasync get(key: string, compute: () => Promise<number>): Promise<number> {\n  // Check existing cache\n  const hit = entries.get(key);\n  if (hit !== undefined && hit.expiresAt > clock.now()) {\n    return hit.value;\n  }\n  \n  // Check if a computation is in-flight for this key\n  const inFlight = inFlightMap.get(key);\n  if (inFlight !== undefined) {\n    return inFlight;\n  }\n  \n  // Start new computation\n  const computation = (async () => {\n    try {\n      const value = await compute();\n      entries.set(key, { value, expiresAt: clock.now() + ttlMs });\n      return value;\n    } finally {\n      inFlightMap.delete(key);\n    }\n  })();\n  \n  inFlightMap.set(key, computation);\n  return computation;\n}\n```\n\nWait, but the contract says \"a FAILED computation is never cached\". With the try/finally above, if compute throws, the entry is never set (good), and the in-flight promise is cleared (good, so retry works).\n\nBut wait \u2014 there's a subtle issue. If compute throws, the in-flight promise rejects. The caller of `get` gets that rejection. But if another caller comes in after the rejection, the in-flight entry is gone (deleted in finally), so they'll start a new computation. That's correct.\n\nBut what if two callers are waiting on the SAME in-flight computation? Both get the same promise. If it rejects, both get rejected. That's fine \u2014 the contract says \"a failed computation is never cached (a later get retries)\". The \"later\" here means after the failure, not during.\n\nActually, let me re-read: \"a FAILED computation is never cached (a later get retries)\". This means if compute throws, the entry is not cached, and a subsequent get call should retry. With my implementation:\n- First get: starts computation, sets in-flight\n- Compute throws: in-flight is cleared, entry not set\n- Second get: no cache hit, no in-flight \u2192 starts new computation \u2192 retries. \u2713\n\nWhat about \"concurrent get calls for the same fresh key share ONE computation\"?\n- Two gets happen simultaneously\n- Both miss cache\n- First one sets in-flight, starts computation\n- Second one sees in-flight, awaits it\n- Both resolve with same value\n- Compute invoked once \u2713\n\nNow let me also think about whether `compute` is called exactly once even when there's a cache hit. Yes \u2014 if cache hit, we return immediately without calling compute.\n\nLet me also make sure the cache.ts types match the Clock interface from lib/clock.ts. The Clock interface is:\n```ts\nexport interface Clock {\n  now(): number;\n}\n```\n\nAnd `createPriceCache` takes `clock: Clock`. Good.\n\nNow let me also think about the tests. The existing tests use `{ now: (): number => 0 }` which is a Clock. Good.\n\nFor the new deferred tests, I need to:\n1. Test that `lineTotal` resolves deferred values correctly\n2. Test that `computeTotal` handles deferred items\n3. Test that `reportLine` and `buildReport` resolve deferred values\n4. Test that `amountLabel` returns \"deferred\" for deferred adjustments\n5. Test the cache deduplication contract\n\nLet me also check: the existing tests for `pricing.test.ts` call `lineTotal` and `computeTotal` synchronously. I need to make them async.\n\nNow let me write the files:\n\n**types.ts**: Add deferred variant\n\n**pricing.ts**: Make lineTotal and computeTotal async\n\n**report.ts**: Make reportLine and buildReport async, handle \"deferred\" label\n\n**cache.ts**: Fix concurrent deduplication and failure handling\n\n**pricing.test.ts**: Update existing tests to be async, add deferred tests\n\nLet me also check if there are any other files that import from pricing.ts or report.ts... Looking at the project files, only `pricing.test.ts` imports from them. The cache.ts is standalone.\n\nWait, I should also check if `computeTotal` is called anywhere else. Looking at the corpus, I don't see any other usages in the provided files. The task says \"Keep the exported names and shapes otherwise stable\".\n\nLet me also think about whether `pctOf` and `adjustmentOf` need changes. They don't \u2014 they just compute the adjustment amount. The async part is only in `lineTotal` where we need to await the deferred load.\n\nActually, I realize I should also consider: should `computeTotal` be `async` or should it await all lineTots? Yes, it should be async and use `Promise.all` or sequential awaits.\n\nLet me write the code now.\n\nFor cache.ts, I need to be careful about the in-flight map. Let me think about edge cases:\n- What if compute takes a long time and the TTL expires during computation? The entry won't be set until compute resolves, so subsequent calls will still see no cache and wait on the in-flight promise. That's fine.\n- What if the clock advances past TTL during computation? The entry won't be set, so when it does resolve, it will be set with `expiresAt = clock.now() + ttlMs`. That's correct.\n\nLet me also think about whether the in-flight map should be cleared on success or just on completion. Using `finally` is correct \u2014 it clears on both success and failure.\n\nOne more thing: the contract says \"concurrent get calls for the same fresh key share ONE computation\". What if compute is called, succeeds, and then a new get comes in before the entry is set? That can't happen because the entry is set synchronously after await. But what if two gets happen, both miss cache, and both start computations? With the in-flight map, only the first one starts a computation; the second waits on the first's promise. So compute is called once. \u2713\n\nWhat if compute throws? The in-flight is cleared, entry not set. A subsequent get will start a new computation. \u2713\n\nOK, I think my design is solid. Let me write the files.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the cache contract more carefully:\n\n\"concurrent get calls for the same fresh key share ONE computation (compute \u2014 and therefore a deferred load() \u2014 is invoked exactly once)\"\n\nSo if compute is async and involves a deferred load, the load is invoked exactly once for concurrent calls. My implementation handles this.\n\n\"a FAILED computation is never cached (a later get retries)\"\n\nIf compute throws, the entry is not cached, and a later get retries. My implementation handles this.\n\n\"entries still expire after the TTL via the injected Clock\"\n\nMy implementation uses clock.now() for expiration. \u2713\n\n\"different keys stay independent\"\n\nMap keys are independent. \u2713\n\nGood. Let me write the code.\n\nOne more consideration: the `compute` function in cache.ts takes `() => Promise<number>`. When we have deferred adjustments, the compute function will call `computeTotal` which is now async. That's fine \u2014 computeTotal returns `Promise<number>`.\n\nLet me also think about whether I need to update the `PriceCache` interface. The interface is:\n```ts\nexport interface PriceCache {\n  get(key: string, compute: () => Promise<number>): Promise<number>;\n}\n```\n\nThis is already async, so no changes needed.\n\nNow let me write all the files.\n\nFor the cache fix, I'll use a WeakMap or just a regular Map for in-flight computations. Since keys are strings, a Map is fine.\n\nActually, I realize I should also think about whether the in-flight map should be part of the cache closure or exposed. It should be private (in the closure).\n\nLet me write the final code:\n\n```ts\n// FILE: types.ts\nexport type Amount =\n  | { readonly kind: \"fixed\"; readonly value: number }\n  | { readonly kind: \"percent\"; readonly pct: number }\n  | { readonly kind: \"deferred\"; readonly load: () => Promise<number> };\n\nexport interface LineItem {\n  readonly id: string;\n  readonly base: number;\n  readonly adjustment: Amount;\n}\n```\n\n```ts\n// FILE: pricing.ts\nimport type { Amount, LineItem } from \"./types\";\n\nexport const PERCENT_BASE = 100;\n\nfunction pctOf(amount: Amount): number {\n  return amount.kind === \"percent\" ? amount.pct : 0;\n}\n\nfunction adjustmentOf(base: number, amount: Amount): number {\n  if (amount.kind === \"fixed\") {\n    return amount.value;\n  }\n  if (amount.kind === \"percent\") {\n    return (base * pctOf(amount)) / PERCENT_BASE;\n  }\n  // deferred \u2014 caller must await load()\n  throw new Error(\"deferred adjustment must be resolved before adjustmentOf\");\n}\n\nexport async function lineTotal(item: LineItem): Promise<number> {\n  const base = item.base;\n  if (item.adjustment.kind === \"deferred\") {\n    const deferredValue = await item.adjustment.load();\n    return base + deferredValue;\n  }\n  return base + adjustmentOf(base, item.adjustment);\n}\n\nexport async function computeTotal(items: readonly LineItem[]): Promise<number> {\n  const totals = await Promise.all(items.map(lineTotal));\n  return totals.reduce((sum: number, total: number): number => sum + total, 0);\n}\n```\n\nWait, I should keep `adjustmentOf` simple. Let me reconsider \u2014 the task says \"Keep every function/method under 20 lines\" and \"Reuse existing utilities\". Let me keep `adjustmentOf` as is but handle deferred in `lineTotal`.\n\nActually, `adjustmentOf` doesn't need to handle deferred because `lineTotal` handles it before calling `adjustmentOf`. But if someone calls `adjustmentOf` with a deferred amount, it should throw or handle it. Let me just keep the original logic and not add the throw \u2014 it's an internal helper.\n\nActually, looking at the original code:\n```ts\nfunction adjustmentOf(base: number, amount: Amount): number {\n  if (amount.kind === \"fixed\") {\n    return amount.value;\n  }\n  return (base * pctOf(amount)) / PERCENT_BASE;\n}\n```\n\nIf `amount.kind === \"deferred\"`, `pctOf` returns 0, so `adjustmentOf` returns 0. That's wrong but it won't be called for deferred amounts in our new code. Let me keep it simple and just handle deferred in `lineTotal`.\n\n```ts\n// FILE: pricing.ts\nimport type { Amount, LineItem } from \"./types\";\n\nexport const PERCENT_BASE = 100;\n\nfunction pctOf(amount: Amount): number {\n  return amount.kind === \"percent\" ? amount.pct : 0;\n}\n\nfunction adjustmentOf(base: number, amount: Amount): number {\n  if (amount.kind === \"fixed\") {\n    return amount.value;\n  }\n  return (base * pctOf(amount)) / PERCENT_BASE;\n}\n\nexport async function lineTotal(item: LineItem): Promise<number> {\n  if (item.adjustment.kind === \"deferred\") {\n    const value = await item.adjustment.load();\n    return item.base + value;\n  }\n  return item.base + adjustmentOf(item.base, item.adjustment);\n}\n\nexport async function computeTotal(items: readonly LineItem[]): Promise<number> {\n  const totals = await Promise.all(items.map(lineTotal));\n  return totals.reduce((sum, total) => sum + total, 0);\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but the task says \"Reuse `Clock` from `../../lib/clock`; never call `Date.now()` in the logic.\" This is for the cache, not pricing. Pricing doesn't use Date.now().\n\nAlso, \"named constants (no magic numbers)\". The `PERCENT_BASE = 100` is already a named constant. Good.\n\nLet me also think about whether I need to handle the case where `load()` throws. The task doesn't specify, but I should let it propagate naturally.\n\n```ts\n// FILE: report.ts\nimport type { Amount, LineItem } from \"./types\";\nimport { lineTotal } from \"./pricing\";\n\nconst KIND_LABEL: Record<Amount[\"kind\"], string> = {\n  fixed: \"flat\",\n  percent: \"rate\",\n  deferred: \"deferred\",\n};\n\nexport function amountLabel(amount: Amount): string {\n  return KIND_LABEL[amount.kind];\n}\n\nexport async function reportLine(item: LineItem): Promise<string> {\n  const total = await lineTotal(item);\n  return `${item.id} [${amountLabel(item.adjustment)}]: ${String(total)}`;\n}\n\nexport async function buildReport(items: readonly LineItem[]): Promise<readonly string[]> {\n  return Promise.all(items.map(reportLine));\n}\n```\n\nWait, the original `KIND_LABEL` uses `Partial<Record<...>>` and has an `UNKNOWN_LABEL`. With the new `deferred` variant, all variants are covered. Let me simplify:\n\n```ts\nconst KIND_LABEL: Record<Amount[\"kind\"], string> = {\n  fixed: \"flat\",\n  percent: \"rate\",\n  deferred: \"deferred\",\n};\n```\n\nThis is exhaustive now. If someone adds a new variant without updating this, TypeScript will error. Good.\n\n```ts\n// FILE: cache.ts\nimport type { Clock } from \"../../lib/clock\";\n\nexport interface PriceCache {\n  get(key: string, compute: () => Promise<number>): Promise<number>;\n}\n\ninterface CacheEntry {\n  readonly value: number;\n  readonly expiresAt: number;\n}\n\nconst DEFAULT_TTL_MS = 60_000;\n\nexport function createPriceCache(clock: Clock, ttlMs: number = DEFAULT_TTL_MS): PriceCache {\n  const entries = new Map<string, CacheEntry>();\n  const inFlight = new Map<string, Promise<number>>();\n\n  return {\n    async get(key: string, compute: () => Promise<number>): Promise<number> {\n      const hit = entries.get(key);\n      if (hit !== undefined && hit.expiresAt > clock.now()) {\n        return hit.value;\n      }\n\n      const pending = inFlight.get(key);\n      if (pending !== undefined) {\n        return pending;\n      }\n\n      const computation = (async (): Promise<number> => {\n        try {\n          const value = await compute();\n          entries.set(key, { value, expiresAt: clock.now() + ttlMs });\n          return value;\n        } finally {\n          inFlight.delete(key);\n        }\n      })();\n\n      inFlight.set(key, computation);\n      return computation;\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\nWait, I need to think about this more carefully. The `compute` function is `() => Promise<number>`. When we call `compute()`, it returns a promise. If we await that promise, we get the value. But if two callers both call `compute()`, we'd invoke it twice.\n\nIn my implementation, I only call `compute()` once per key (the first caller). Subsequent callers await the same promise. That's correct.\n\nBut wait, there's a subtle issue: what if `compute()` itself is called multiple times? In my implementation, `compute()` is called once, and the result is cached. But what if the computation takes a long time and the TTL expires? The entry won't be set until the computation completes, so subsequent calls will still see no cache and wait on the in-flight promise. That's correct.\n\nWhat if the computation throws? The `finally` block clears the in-flight entry, and the entry is not set. A subsequent call will start a new computation. That's correct.\n\nBut there's another issue: what if two callers call `get` simultaneously, both miss the cache, and both check `inFlight` before either sets it? Then both would start computations. Let me think...\n\nIn JavaScript, the event loop is single-threaded. So between the `inFlight.get(key)` check and the `inFlight.set(key, computation)` call,</think>// FILE: types.ts\nexport type Amount =\n  | { readonly kind: \"fixed\"; readonly value: number }\n  | { readonly kind: \"percent\"; readonly pct: number }\n  | { readonly kind: \"deferred\"; readonly load: () => Promise<number> };\n\nexport interface LineItem {\n  readonly id: string;\n  readonly base: number;\n  readonly adjustment: Amount;\n}\n\n// FILE: pricing.ts\nimport type { Amount, LineItem } from \"./types\";\n\nexport const PERCENT_BASE = 100;\n\nfunction pctOf(amount: Amount): number {\n  return amount.kind === \"percent\" ? amount.pct : 0;\n}\n\nfunction adjustmentOf(base: number, amount: Amount): number {\n  if (amount.kind === \"fixed\") {\n    return amount.value;\n  }\n  return (base * pctOf(amount)) / PERCENT_BASE;\n}\n\nexport async function lineTotal(item: LineItem): Promise<number> {\n  if (item.adjustment.kind === \"deferred\") {\n    const value = await item.adjustment.load();\n    return item.base + value;\n  }\n  return item.base + adjustmentOf(item.base, item.adjustment);\n}\n\nexport async function computeTotal(items: readonly LineItem[]): Promise<number> {\n  const totals = await Promise.all(items.map(lineTotal));\n  return totals.reduce((sum: number, total: number): number => sum + total, 0);\n}\n\n// FILE: report.ts\nimport type { Amount, LineItem } from \"./types\";\nimport { lineTotal } from \"./pricing\";\n\nconst KIND_LABEL: Record<Amount[\"kind\"], string> = {\n  fixed: \"flat\",\n  percent: \"rate\",\n  deferred: \"deferred\",\n};\n\nexport function amountLabel(amount: Amount): string {\n  return KIND_LABEL[amount.kind];\n}\n\nexport async function reportLine(item: LineItem): Promise<string> {\n  const total = await lineTotal(item);\n  return `${item.id} [${amountLabel(item.adjustment)}]: ${String(total)}`;\n}\n\nexport async function buildReport(items: readonly LineItem[]): Promise<readonly string[]> {\n  return Promise.all(items.map(reportLine));\n}\n\n// FILE: cache.ts\nimport type { Clock } from \"../../lib/clock\";\n\nexport interface PriceCache {\n  /** Return the cached total for `key` if fresh, else run `compute` and cache its result. */\n  get(key: string, compute: () => Promise<number>): Promise<number>;\n}\n\ninterface CacheEntry {\n  readonly value: number;\n  readonly expiresAt: number;\n}\n\nconst DEFAULT_TTL_MS = 60_000;\n\nexport function createPriceCache(clock: Clock, ttlMs: number = DEFAULT_TTL_MS): PriceCache {\n  const entries = new Map<string, CacheEntry>();\n  const inFlight = new Map<string, Promise<number>>();\n\n  return {\n    async get(key: string, compute: () => Promise<number>): Promise<number> {\n      const hit = entries.get(key);\n      if (hit !== undefined && hit.expiresAt > clock.now()) {\n        return hit.value;\n      }\n\n      const pending = inFlight.get(key);\n      if (pending !== undefined) {\n        return pending;\n      }\n\n      const computation = (async (): Promise<number> => {\n        try {\n          const value = await compute();\n          entries.set(key, { value, expiresAt: clock.now() + ttlMs });\n          return value;\n        } finally {\n          inFlight.delete(key);\n        }\n      })();\n\n      inFlight.set(key, computation);\n      return computation;\n    },\n  };\n}\n\n// FILE: pricing.test.ts\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { computeTotal, lineTotal } from \"./pricing\";\nimport { amountLabel, buildReport, reportLine } from \"./report\";\nimport { createPriceCache } from \"./cache\";\n\ndescribe(\"pricing\", () => {\n  it(\"should add the fixed value when the adjustment is fixed\", async () => {\n    expect(\n      await lineTotal({ id: \"a\", base: 100, adjustment: { kind: \"fixed\", value: 20 } })\n    ).toBe(120);\n  });\n  it(\"should apply the rate when the adjustment is a percent\", async () => {\n    expect(\n      await lineTotal({ id: \"b\", base: 200, adjustment: { kind: \"percent\", pct: 10 } })\n    ).toBe(220);\n  });\n  it(\"should resolve the deferred load and add it when the adjustment is deferred\", async () => {\n    expect(\n      await lineTotal({\n        id: \"c\",\n        base: 100,\n        adjustment: { kind: \"deferred\", load: async () => 25 },\n      })\n    ).toBe(125);\n  });\n  it(\"should sum the line totals when computing an order total\", async () => {\n    const items = [\n      { id: \"a\", base: 100, adjustment: { kind: \"fixed\", value: 20 } },\n      { id: \"b\", base: 200, adjustment: { kind: \"percent\", pct: 10 } },\n    ] as const;\n    expect(await computeTotal(items)).toBe(340);\n  });\n  it(\"should compute the total when items include a deferred adjustment\", async () => {\n    const items = [\n      { id: \"a\", base: 100, adjustment: { kind: \"fixed\", value: 10 } },\n      {\n        id: \"b\",\n        base: 50,\n        adjustment: { kind: \"deferred\", load: async () => 5 },\n      },\n    ] as const;\n    expect(await computeTotal(items)).toBe(115);\n  });\n  it(\"should await concurrent deferred loads when computing a total\", async () => {\n    let calls = 0;\n    const items = [\n      {\n        id: \"a\",\n        base: 10,\n        adjustment: { kind: \"deferred\", load: async () => { calls += 1; return 1; } },\n      },\n      {\n        id: \"b\",\n        base: 20,\n        adjustment: { kind: \"deferred\", load: async () => { calls += 1; return 2; } },\n      },\n    ] as const;\n    const total = await computeTotal(items);\n    expect(total).toBe(33);\n    expect(calls).toBe(2);\n  });\n  it(\"should propagate a rejected deferred load\", async () => {\n    const items = [\n      {\n        id: \"a\",\n        base: 10,\n        adjustment: { kind: \"deferred\", load: async () => { throw new Error(\"boom\"); } },\n      },\n    ] as const;\n    await expect(computeTotal(items)).rejects.toThrow(\"boom\");\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"report\", () => {\n  it(\"should label each adjustment when building the report\", () => {\n    expect(amountLabel({ kind: \"fixed\", value: 5 })).toBe(\"flat\");\n    expect(amountLabel({ kind: \"percent\", pct: 5 })).toBe(\"rate\");\n    expect(amountLabel({ kind: \"deferred\", load: async () => 0 })).toBe(\"deferred\");\n  });\n  it(\"should render one line per item when building the report\", async () => {\n    const lines = await buildReport([\n      { id: \"a\", base: 100, adjustment: { kind: \"fixed\", value: 20 } },\n    ]);\n    expect(lines).toEqual([\"a [flat]: 120\"]);\n  });\n  it(\"should render the resolved total for a deferred adjustment\", async () => {\n    const lines = await buildReport([\n      {\n        id: \"b\",\n        base: 50,\n        adjustment: { kind: \"deferred\", load: async () => 7 },\n      },\n    ]);\n    expect(lines).toEqual([\"b [deferred]: 57\"]);\n  });\n  it(\"should render mixed adjustment kinds in one report\", async () => {\n    const lines = await buildReport([\n      { id: \"a\", base: 100, adjustment: { kind: \"fixed\", value: 10 } },\n      { id: \"b\", base: 200, adjustment: { kind: \"percent\", pct: 5 } },\n      {\n        id: \"c\",\n        base: 30,\n        adjustment: { kind: \"deferred\", load: async () => 3 },\n      },\n    ]);\n    expect(lines).toEqual([\"a [flat]: 110\", \"b [rate]: 210\", \"c [deferred]: 33\"]);\n  });\n  it(\"should not stringified-promise in the report line\", async () => {\n    const lines = await buildReport([\n      {\n        id: \"a\",\n        base: 0,\n        adjustment: { kind: \"deferred\", load: async () => 42 },\n      },\n    ]);\n    expect(lines[0]).not.toContain(\"Promise\");\n    expect(lines[0]).toContain(\"42\");\n  });\n  it(\"should propagate a rejected deferred load through reportLine\", async () => {\n    await expect(\n      reportLine({\n        id: \"a\",\n        base: 0,\n        adjustment: { kind: \"deferred\", load: async () => { throw new Error(\"x\"); } },\n      })\n    ).rejects.toThrow(\"x\");\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"createPriceCache\", () => {\n  it(\"should reuse the cached total when the entry is still fresh\", async () => {\n    let calls = 0;\n    const cache = createPriceCache({ now: (): number => 0 }, 1000);\n    const compute = async (): Promise<number> => {\n      calls += 1;\n      return 42;\n    };\n    expect(await cache.get(\"k\", compute)).toBe(42);\n    expect(await cache.get(\"k\", compute)).toBe(42);\n    expect(calls).toBe(1);\n  });\n  it(\"should recompute the total when the entry has expired\", async () => {\n    let t = 0;\n    let calls = 0;\n    const cache = createPriceCache({ now: (): number => t }, 1000);\n    const compute = async (): Promise<number> => {\n      calls += 1;\n      return calls;\n    };\n    expect(await cache.get(\"k\", compute)).toBe(1);\n    t = 1500;\n    expect(await cache.get(\"k\", compute)).toBe(2);\n    expect(calls).toBe(2);\n  });\n  it(\"should invoke compute exactly once for concurrent get calls on the same fresh key\", async () => {\n    let calls = 0;\n    const cache = createPriceCache({ now: (): number => 0 }, 1000);\n    const compute = async (): Promise<number> => {\n      calls += 1;\n      await new Promise<void>(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 10));\n      return 99;\n    };\n    const [a, b, c] = await Promise.all([\n      cache.get(\"k\", compute),\n      cache.get(\"k\", compute),\n      cache.get(\"k\", compute),\n    ]);\n    expect(a).toBe(99);\n    expect(b).toBe(99);\n    expect(c).toBe(99);\n    expect(calls).toBe(1);\n  });\n  it(\"should never cache a failed computation so a later get retries\", async () => {\n    let calls = 0;\n    const cache = createPriceCache({ now: (): number => 0 }, 1000);\n    const compute = async (): Promise<number> => {\n      calls += 1;\n      if (calls === 1) throw new Error(\"transient\");\n      return 7;\n    };\n    await expect(cache.get(\"k\", compute)).rejects.toThrow(\"transient\");\n    const value = await cache.get(\"k\", compute);\n    expect(value).toBe(7);\n    expect(calls).toBe(2);\n  });\n  it(\"should expire entries after the TTL via the injected Clock\", async () => {\n    let t = 0;\n    let calls = 0;\n    const cache = createPriceCache({ now: (): number => t }, 100);\n    const compute = async (): Promise<number> => {\n      calls += 1;\n      return calls;\n    };\n    expect(await cache.get(\"k\", compute)).toBe(1);\n    t = 50;\n    expect(await cache.get(\"k\", compute)).toBe(1);\n    t = 150;\n    expect(await cache.get(\"k\", compute)).toBe(2);\n    expect(calls).toBe(2);\n  });\n  it(\"should keep different keys independent\", async () => {\n    let callsA = 0;\n    let callsB = 0;\n    const cache = createPriceCache({ now: (): number => 0 }, 1000);\n    const computeA = async (): Promise<number> => { callsA += 1; return 1; };\n    const computeB = async (): Promise<number> => { callsB += 1; return 2; };\n    expect(await cache.get(\"a\", computeA)).toBe(1);\n    expect(await cache.get(\"b\", computeB)).toBe(2);\n    expect(await cache.get(\"a\", computeA)).toBe(1);\n    expect(callsA).toBe(1);\n    expect(callsB).toBe(1);\n  });\n  it(\"should share one deferred load() across concurrent get calls\", async () => {\n    let loadCalls = 0;\n    const cache = createPriceCache({ now: (): number => 0 }, 1000);\n    const compute = async (): Promise<number> => {\n      const value = await new Promise<number>(resolve => setTimeout(() => {\n        loadCalls += 1;\n        resolve(loadCalls);\n      }, 10));\n      return value * 10;\n    };\n    const [a, b] = await Promise.all([cache.get(\"k\", compute), cache.get(\"k\", compute)]);\n    expect(a).toBe(10);\n    expect(b).toBe(10);\n    expect(loadCalls).toBe(1);\n  });\n  it(\"should retry after a failed deferred load\", async () => {\n    let loadCalls = 0;\n    const cache = createPriceCache({ now: (): number => 0 }, 1000);\n    const compute = async (): Promise<number> => {\n      loadCalls += 1;\n      if (loadCalls === 1) throw new Error(\"nope\");\n      return 5;\n    };\n    await expect(cache.get(\"k\", compute)).rejects.toThrow(\"nope\");\n    const value = await cache.get(\"k\", compute);\n    expect(value).toBe(5);\n    expect(loadCalls).toBe(2);\n  });\n});", "prompt_tokens": 84425, "completion_tokens": 7571, "total_tokens": 91996, "cached_tokens": 0, "think_tokens": 4095, "answer_tokens": 3476, "prefill_tps": 1885.7196067955772, "decode_tps": 76.96775392692358, "prompt_n": 84425, "prompt_ms": 44770.707, "predicted_n": 7571, "predicted_ms": 98365.869, "prompt_per_token_ms": 0.5303015339058336, "predicted_per_token_ms": 12.99245396909259, "draft_n": null, "draft_n_accepted": null, "draft_acceptance_pct": null, "ttft_s": 44.938, "ttfa_s": 97.95, "think_time_s": 53.013, "latency_s": 143.307, "finish_reason": "stop", "think_closed": true, "has_answer": true, "truncated_thinking": false, "sampling": {"temperature": 0